<https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/evidence-meta-sign-2012#visual-item> a crm:E36_Visual_Item;
    rdfs:label "An illuminated Italian shop sign reading META SUPERNEGOZI at night";
    la:digitally_shown_by <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/images/meta-sign-2012.jpg>.
mca:evidence-meta-sign-2012 crm:P129_is_about mca:concept-metaconceptual-art;
    crm:P138i_has_representation <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/evidence-meta-sign-2012#visual-item>;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "One digital photograph, held by the studio, of an illuminated shop sign photographed at night. The sign carries a red oval with the word META in white capitals, with a grave accent on the final letter, and beneath it the word SUPERNEGOZI; to its right, cropped by the frame, stand the large illuminated letters S, U, P. The wording is Italian: supernegozi means superstores, and meta with that accent is the Italian word for half. The camera's Exif records the capture as 2012-02-27 21:41:53, with no location data. The studio states that the photograph was taken in Italy and that it photographed the sign because the word answered to Metaconceptual Art, the movement concept it had named; that reading is the studio's account, given in 2026, and the photograph records a sign belonging to a business unconnected with the studio.";
    crm:P1_is_identified_by _:b0_r16b0, _:b0_r16b1;
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300026906, aat:300055863, aat:300215302;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by mca:evidence-archived-websites-2013-2016, mca:evidence-museum-correspondence-2013, _:b0_r16b2, _:b0_r16b3, _:b0_r16b4, _:b0_r16b5, _:b0_r16b6;
    crm:P94i_was_created_by _:b0_r16b7;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object;
    rdfs:label "Evidence archive: a photograph of a shop sign reading META, Italy (27 February 2012)";
    la:member_of mca:collection.
mca:evidence-museum-correspondence-2013 crm:P104_is_subject_to _:b0_r17b0;
    crm:P129_is_about mca:art-market-reform, mca:auction-as-artwork, mca:concept-metaconceptual-art, mca:wtf-is-ansel-adams;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Sun & Rain Works holds an email thread of 2013-09-12 between the studio principal and the Jordan Schnitzer Museum of Art at the University of Oregon. Writing after visiting the museum earlier that day, the studio principal set out the project in his own words: that he had created a field of contemporary art called Metaconceptual Art; that 'Art Market Reform' and 'Art Tax' were Metaconceptual Art works; that his art business was named Sun & Rain Works, at which he was Chief Executive Artist; that Metaconceptual Art had eight defining characteristics, which he described as already findable publicly at that date; and that in May 2009 he had auctioned lot 16 of a Sotheby's New York photograph auction, Ansel Adams's 'San Francisco from Twin Peaks', as what he called a performance act of Metaconceptual Art. He invited the museum to distribute and share the material freely. A member of the museum's staff replied the same afternoon, thanking him and saying she would forward it to their staff. This record evidences that the term Metaconceptual Art, its attachment to named works, and the claim to the 2009 Sotheby's consignment were all set down in writing and delivered to an art institution on 2013-09-12 - thirteen years before this site published them as linked data. It is the artist's own declaration, not an independent finding, and the museum's reply is an acknowledgement of receipt only.";
    crm:P1_is_identified_by _:b0_r17b1, _:b0_r17b2;
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300026087, aat:300026906, aat:300055863;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by mca:art-tax, _:b0_r17b3, _:b0_r17b4, _:b0_r17b5, _:b0_r17b6, _:b0_r17b7, _:b0_r17b8;
    crm:P94i_was_created_by _:b0_r17b9;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object;
    rdfs:label "Evidence archive: correspondence declaring Metaconceptual Art to a university art museum (2013)";
    la:member_of mca:collection.
mca:evidence-pastebin-2011-2012 crm:P104_is_subject_to _:b0_r18b0;
    crm:P129_is_about mca:concept-metaconceptual-art, mca:eight-sentences;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Two public postings on the text-hosting service Pastebin, made under the account LIVINGART8 and still publicly readable, carry Metaconceptual Art before 2013. The earlier, titled 'The Art World is Flat', was posted on 2011-11-28 at 07:51:51 CDT. It presents a poem which its author introduces as a metaconceptual Art poem written some years earlier and published free to the world that day in honour of Cyber Monday 2011, and it links to a Sun & Rain Works page on Facebook. The later, titled 'MetaConceptual Art', was posted on 2012-11-22 at 23:19:57 CDT. Headed '#WTF is MetaConceptual Art ?!' and carrying the same Facebook link, it sets out 'The 8 Defining characteristics of Metaconceptual Art' as a numbered list of eight sentences. Those eight sentences are the work catalogued in this collection as Eight Sentences On Metaconceptual Art, and they agree with the published text word for word, differing only in a comma, a space, and the spelling of 'Transcendance', later normalised to 'Transcendence'. These postings establish that the term, the eight-part definition, and the studio's attachment of both to Sun & Rain Works were public and fixed in a third party's records on 2012-11-22, and that the term was already in use as a settled description on 2011-11-28.";
    crm:P1_is_identified_by _:b0_r18b1, _:b0_r18b2;
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300026087, aat:300026906, aat:300055863;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by _:b0_r18b3, _:b0_r18b4, _:b0_r18b5, _:b0_r18b6, _:b0_r18b7, _:b0_r18b8, _:b0_r18b9;
    crm:P94i_was_created_by _:b0_r18b10;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object;
    rdfs:label "Evidence archive: public postings of Metaconceptual Art (2011-2012)", "Evidence archive: the 2011-2012 postings";
    la:member_of mca:collection.
<https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/evidence-postcard-work-2008#visual-item> a crm:E36_Visual_Item;
    rdfs:label "A museum postcard painted over and inscribed 'For the Love of Art', framed under glass";
    la:digitally_shown_by <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/images/for-the-love-of-art-postcard-2008.jpg>.
mca:evidence-postcard-work-2008 crm:P129_is_about mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    crm:P138i_has_representation <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/evidence-postcard-work-2008#visual-item>;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "One digital photograph, held by the studio, of a small work made on a commercial museum postcard and shown framed under glass. The picture side has been painted over in acrylic and ink with a single large flower in yellow and orange over a purple ground, with green leaves below, and is signed in ink 'Joe Chirum' and dated '08'. The address side carries a handwritten inscription in ink: '\"For the Love of Art, these tulips are beautiful! Almost as good as in real life\"', followed by the initials 'J.M.C.' and '08', and a postscript, 'P.S. \"Double the Fun\"'. The printed matter of the postcard remains visible beneath the writing and identifies it as one of a series of Impressionist painting postcards published by an art museum in Chicago, reproducing a painting of tulips by Cezanne. The photograph was taken on 2011-11-16 by the same camera phone that took the photograph of the sheet inscribed 'W.T.F. is Art ?' eight days earlier. The studio dates the work itself to 2008, which is the date written on it; the photograph shows only that the work existed by November 2011.";
    crm:P1_is_identified_by _:b0_r19b0, _:b0_r19b1;
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300026906, aat:300055863, aat:300215302;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by mca:evidence-inscription-photograph-2011, _:b0_r19b2, _:b0_r19b3, _:b0_r19b4, _:b0_r19b5, _:b0_r19b6;
    crm:P94i_was_created_by _:b0_r19b7;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object;
    rdfs:label "Evidence archive: a painted postcard inscribed 'For the Love of Art' and signed 2008 (photographed 2011)";
    la:member_of mca:collection.
mca:evidence-saturday-market-membership crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "A statement made by the Eugene Saturday Market, from its own membership records, that Sun and Rain Works was a member of the market in 2005, 2006 and 2008. The studio wrote to the market on 2024-09-03 asking for access to its historical membership records and receipts; the market's general manager replied on 2024-09-06 with those three years. The Eugene Saturday Market, in Eugene, Oregon, has run weekly since 9 May 1970 and describes itself as the oldest continuously operating weekly open-air crafts market in the United States; its rule is that goods are made and sold by the person who made them. Membership is therefore vendor membership, and what the market's records attest is that a business under this name was making and selling work at its stalls in those years. This is the earliest attestation of the name Sun and Rain Works in this collection, and the only one made by a party other than the studio about a date before 2011.";
    crm:P1_is_identified_by _:b0_r20b0, _:b0_r20b1;
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300026906, aat:300055863;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by _:b0_r20b2, _:b0_r20b3, _:b0_r20b4, _:b0_r20b5;
    crm:P94i_was_created_by _:b0_r20b6;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object;
    rdfs:label "Evidence archive: Sun and Rain Works in a market's membership records (2005, 2006, 2008)";
    la:member_of mca:collection.
mca:evidence-sfmoma-approach-2008 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "An email of 2008-08-11 at 16:01, sent by the studio principal to the photography department of the San Francisco Museum of Modern Art, under the subject 'Ansel Adams Finds Art in SanFrancisco'. It introduces its writer as an artist living in Eugene, Oregon and sole proprietor of an art business named Sun and Rain Works; describes the recent acquisition of a previously unknown Ansel Adams photograph of San Francisco, which the writer then believed to date from 1946; states that he owns it and is researching its historical and artistic significance; reports that he is in contact with people on the Portland art scene willing to appraise it, and with a named individual at an institution he calls the Ansel Adams Museum in San Francisco; says he thought this happening might be of some interest to the museum; describes attached photographs made with a 1.3 megapixel FujiFilm camera; and closes 'For the Love of Art'. It is three days earlier than the approach to Sotheby's, carries the same subject line, and repeats its opening paragraph almost word for word. It is no longer the earliest contemporaneous document in this collection: two messages of 2008-08-08 to the Ansel Adams organisation precede it by two days. It remains the earliest contemporaneous use of the name Sun and Rain Works, which neither earlier message carries. It should not be confused with the earliest attested date for the business, which is earlier and rests on a different kind of source: a market has stated from its own membership records that the business was trading at its stalls in 2005. That statement was made in 2024 about 2005; this letter was written in 2008 and uses the name itself.";
    crm:P1_is_identified_by _:b0_r21b0, _:b0_r21b1;
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300026906, aat:300055863;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by mca:evidence-adams-gallery-2008, mca:evidence-sothebys-approach-2008, _:b0_r21b10, _:b0_r21b11, _:b0_r21b12, _:b0_r21b13, _:b0_r21b14, _:b0_r21b15, _:b0_r21b16, _:b0_r21b2, _:b0_r21b3, _:b0_r21b4, _:b0_r21b5, _:b0_r21b6, _:b0_r21b7, _:b0_r21b8, _:b0_r21b9;
    crm:P94i_was_created_by _:b0_r21b17;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object;
    rdfs:label "Evidence archive: the approach to a museum photography department (11 August 2008)";
    la:member_of mca:collection.
<https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/evidence-sothebys-approach-2008#visual-item> a crm:E36_Visual_Item;
    rdfs:label "The Ansel Adams print composed with The Philosophy of Andy Warhol";
    la:digitally_shown_by <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/images/adams-warhol-2008.jpg>.
mca:evidence-sothebys-approach-2008 crm:P138i_has_representation <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/evidence-sothebys-approach-2008#visual-item>;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "A thread of eleven messages between the studio principal and a junior cataloguer in the Photographs department at Sotheby's, New York, running from 2008-08-14 to 2009-03-19, together with the 3 MB attachment sent with the first of them. The studio's opening message introduces its author as an artist living in Eugene, Oregon and sole proprietor of an art business named Sun and Rain Works, states that he owns an Ansel Adams print and is researching its historical and artistic significance, describes the attached photographs as made with a 1.3 megapixel FujiFilm camera, and is signed off 'For the Love of Art'. Sotheby's replies the next day offering to include the print in its April 2009 sale at a provisional estimate of $7,000 to $10,000, gives a consignment deadline of end of January 2009, names its department administrator, and attaches its consignment charges. A month later the cataloguer writes again to ask whether the owner wishes to proceed, naming the work as 'San Francisco from Twin Peaks'. The studio answers the following day that it is interested in consigning, that it is in Hawai'i at the time, and that it will arrange shipment itself. The attachment is held and has been examined; the messages are held as screenshots of a mail client. Two further messages, of 2009-03-11 and 2009-03-12, follow the consignment through to the sale: the department's administrator writes that the catalogue has gone out and that the signed consignment agreement posted in early February has not come back, and the studio replies that the consignment sheet was received and will be faxed, giving a general-delivery address in Eugene and explaining a delay in mail forwarded to Maui.";
    crm:P1_is_identified_by _:b0_r22b0, _:b0_r22b1;
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300026906, aat:300055863;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by mca:evidence-adams-gallery-2008, mca:evidence-consignment-agreement-2009, mca:evidence-consignment-photographs-2008, mca:evidence-saturday-market-membership, mca:evidence-sfmoma-approach-2008, _:b0_r22b10, _:b0_r22b11, _:b0_r22b12, _:b0_r22b13, _:b0_r22b14, _:b0_r22b15, _:b0_r22b16, _:b0_r22b17, _:b0_r22b18, _:b0_r22b19, _:b0_r22b2, _:b0_r22b20, _:b0_r22b21, _:b0_r22b22, _:b0_r22b23, _:b0_r22b24, _:b0_r22b25, _:b0_r22b26, _:b0_r22b27, _:b0_r22b28, _:b0_r22b3, _:b0_r22b4, _:b0_r22b5, _:b0_r22b6, _:b0_r22b7, _:b0_r22b8, _:b0_r22b9;
    crm:P94i_was_created_by _:b0_r22b29;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object;
    rdfs:label "Evidence archive: the Sotheby's consignment correspondence (2008-2009)", "Evidence archive: the approach to an auction house (14 August 2008)", "Evidence archive: the studio's first approach to Sotheby's (August 2008)";
    la:member_of mca:collection.
mca:evidence-staged-photographs-2008 crm:P129_is_about mca:wtf-is-ansel-adams;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Seventeen digital photographs, held by the studio, that follow the framed Ansel Adams print 'San Francisco from Twin Peaks' through a sequence: a person posing at a door with a ring of keys; the same person inside a caged storage unit, lifting the print; the print presented to the camera in storage corridors and propped against the railing of a warehouse mezzanine; two frames of the stamped and annotated verso; an older man at a framing workbench taking the print out of its frame and examining it; and three exteriors of a shed and carport. The camera recorded no capture date. The files were rewritten by QuickTime 7.5.5 under Mac OS X 10.5.5 at 2008-11-25 18:56 by that computer's clock, a processing that cannot be earlier than 2008-09-15, when Mac OS X 10.5.5 was released, and they were copied into the studio's working folder on 2009-01-20 by the file system's record. They are therefore a product of the autumn of 2008, or of January 2009 at the latest: before the consignment agreement was executed and before the sale of 30 March 2009. Sun & Rain Works states that they were made by its art assistants as documentation of WTF is Ansel Adams. They were not among the photographs sent to the museum or to the auction house, and the images themselves are not published.";
    crm:P1_is_identified_by _:b0_r23b0, _:b0_r23b1;
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300026906, aat:300055863, aat:300215302;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by mca:evidence-consignment-photographs-2008, mca:evidence-sothebys-approach-2008, _:b0_r23b10, _:b0_r23b11, _:b0_r23b12, _:b0_r23b2, _:b0_r23b3, _:b0_r23b4, _:b0_r23b5, _:b0_r23b6, _:b0_r23b7, _:b0_r23b8, _:b0_r23b9;
    crm:P94i_was_created_by _:b0_r23b13;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object;
    rdfs:label "Evidence archive: the staged photographs of the print (autumn 2008)";
    la:member_of mca:collection.
mca:evidence-wtfia-2009 crm:P104_is_subject_to _:b0_r24b0;
    crm:P129_is_about mca:art-market-reform, mca:wtfisart;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Sun & Rain Works holds the WTFIA folder: the working source material it assembled for its website www.wtfisart.com, comprising digital files dated 2008 to 2009. As held, the folder contains four Microsoft Word documents together with associated sets of reference images. The documents are: 'IsItArt.doc', a fourteen-page compilation of roughly four thousand words of excerpts and source links drawn from art-market press published between 2007 and 2009, covering museum deaccessioning, the contraction of the auction market, art-focused hedge funds, and art treated as an asset class, with material attributed to The New York Times, The Daily Telegraph, the Financial Times, The Nation, The Guardian, Deutsche Welle, CathNews, Livemint, The New York Sun, Portfolio.com, and the Los Angeles Times; 'Photographers.doc', a one-page biographical note on the photographer Lee Balterman; and two dealer-network directories built as the site's link content, 'OldMasters.doc', an eight-page directory of Old Master dealers and galleries with their web addresses, and 'Modern.doc', a directory of modern and contemporary galleries in New York, Florida, and California. The accompanying images comprise seventeen art-market and art-trade pictures dated 2009-03-23, showing auction houses, collectors, art fairs, and finance motifs; sixteen art-historical reference plates dated between 2009-01-21 and 2009-01-26; two paintings of the cleansing of the temple dated 2009-01-22; and thirty photographic files dated between 2008-08-07 and 2009-01-21. Together these evidence the material Sun & Rain Works assembled for www.wtfisart.com, and a dated 2008-2009 compilation activity concerned with the art market, the dealer network, and art history. The folder holds no HTML, so it does not by itself record what the website published or when it went live, and it establishes no precedence over any later artist, work, or event.";
    crm:P1_is_identified_by _:b0_r24b1, _:b0_r24b2;
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300026087, aat:300026906, aat:300055863;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by mca:wtfisart-reconstruction, _:b0_r24b3, _:b0_r24b4, _:b0_r24b5, _:b0_r24b6, _:b0_r24b7;
    crm:P94i_was_created_by _:b0_r24b8;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object;
    rdfs:label "Evidence archive: the WTFIA source folder for www.wtfisart.com (2008-2009)", "The WTFIA source documents, January and March 2009";
    la:member_of mca:collection.
