_:b0_r0b2 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Art Market Reform";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r0b3 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "work:art-market-reform";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r0b4 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "A visual anchor for the project: art, price, reform, and institutional display held together as one unstable field.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r0b5 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The work exists as an object, and the image published here is a photograph of it. The studio states, on 2026-09-22, that Art Market Reform exists and is held in its own collection, and that the file published with this record is a photograph of that physical work rather than a born-digital image. That is the studio's statement about its own holdings, recorded as such; nobody outside the studio has examined the object, and no measurement, medium, support or date of making is recorded here because none has been supplied. Two consequences should be read off it. The first is that this record's modelling is provisional and known to be incomplete: it types the work as a digital object classified as a digital image, which describes the photograph and not the thing photographed, and a fuller record would describe the object and treat this file as documentation of it. That correction is deferred until the object's particulars are to hand, and is noted here so that a reader does not take the present typing for a considered claim about what the work is. The second is that the photograph's own date is not established: the file as published carries no camera metadata, having been re-encoded, so it dates neither the object nor the photographing.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r0b6 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Named, and called a title, in a letter to a museum on 2013-09-12. The studio's letter to the Jordan Schnitzer Museum of Art introduces two works by name: 'Two popular examples of Metaconceptual Art, which are ongoing through time and space as well, are titled \"Art Market Reform\" , and \"Art Tax\"'. It then glosses this one: 'Promoting Art Tax on the local, state, national, and international levels to encourage and promote accountability, transparency, and regulation in the Art Market and Art World.' That is the earliest dated naming of this work held in this collection, it is in the artist's own words, and it was delivered to a named institution which produced its own copy from its archive in 2026, so the date does not rest on the studio's mailbox alone. Three limits go with it. The letter attests that the title was in use on that date; it does not date the making of the image published with this record, and its own phrase, that the works are 'ongoing through time and space', describes them as continuing rather than finished. The letter attaches images but names none of them, so nothing in it identifies the file published here as the thing it names. And a title used in 2013 says nothing about 2009 or before.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r0b7 crm:P14_carried_out_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    crm:P4_has_time-span _:b0_r0b8;
    a crm:E65_Creation;
    rdfs:label "Creation of Art Market Reform".
_:b0_r0b8 crm:P82a_begin_of_the_begin "2013-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    crm:P82b_end_of_the_end "2013-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    a crm:E52_Time-Span;
    rdfs:label "2013".
_:b0_r10b0 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence archive: the studio's websites as captured by the Internet Archive (2013-2016)";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r10b1 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "record:evidence-archived-websites-2013-2016";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r10b10 crm:P14_carried_out_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    crm:P4_has_time-span _:b0_r10b11;
    a crm:E65_Creation;
    rdfs:label "Compilation of the archived-websites record".
_:b0_r10b11 crm:P82a_begin_of_the_begin "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    crm:P82b_end_of_the_end "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    a crm:E52_Time-Span;
    rdfs:label "2026".
_:b0_r10b2 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence status: third-party captures, publicly checkable, and the strongest kind of source in this collection short of an institution's own file. The captures are held by the Internet Archive at https://web.archive.org/web/20141218140553/http://wtfisart.com/ , https://web.archive.org/web/20160111165857/http://wtfisart.com/ , https://web.archive.org/web/20160126080729/http://www.sunandrainworks.com/ and https://web.archive.org/web/20160327052433/http://sunandrainworks.com/content/beginnings-art-odyssey . The capture timestamps are the Archive's own and are not the studio's to set; that is what makes them evidence. Two things inside the pages are not: the content management system's 'Submitted by' stamps, which a site owner can set or change, and the page text itself, which is the studio's writing. So the dates to cite are the capture dates, and the stamps are reported as what the site said of itself.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r10b3 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What the wtfisart.com captures show, and how little it is. The earliest capture of the domain, 2011-02-10, is a parked advertising page with no studio content. It says nothing about 2009: the registrar's receipts record shows the domain was registered for one year on 2009-03-16 and not renewed in the year that followed, so by that capture it had lapsed, and registrars commonly park a lapsed address. The studio registered the name again on 2011-10-16, eight months after this capture, which is why the captures below resume. On 2013-07-20 the domain carries one line: 'Under Construction:Check back soon !'. By 2014-12-18, and still on 2016-01-11, it carries a Drupal installation whose main menu reads 'Artists', 'Art Market Reform is a happening' and 'Got Provenance ?'. The body text is the same paragraph repeated in three panels, ending 'For the Love of God, We are the Art. Long Live the Art Spirit !', and the page states 'Welcome to wtfisart.com. No front page content has been created yet.' That last sentence should be quoted by anyone citing this record. What was captured is a demonstration installation carrying the studio's slogans in its menu, not a working publication. Its value is narrow and real: on a date fixed by a third party, the studio was publicly calling a work of its own a happening, and was publishing the line that is handwritten on the Art Notes board.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r10b4 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What the sunandrainworks.com captures show. The root capture of 2013-05-22 is a bare Drupal installation with no content. By 2016-01-26 the front page carries two posts, and the page 'Beginnings of an Art Odyssey', captured separately on 2016-03-27, sets out all eight defining characteristics of Metaconceptual Art in full: the returning of the formless to form; the commodification of art and its relation to the global economic crisis; the power structures of the art world and its institutions; an archaeology of the art world and art history; the interplay of time and space; transcendence of medium to arrive within the collective consciousness; the annihilating of the boundaries between art-world insiders and outsiders; and the annihilating of the boundaries between art and life. This is the same set, in the same order and substantially the same words, as the posting of 2012 recorded in the postings evidence record, which is itself independently preserved. The site's own stamps give 'Submitted by Rainbow on Sun, 10/04/2015' for that page and 'Fri, 11/14/2014' for its sibling; those are the site's account of itself, and the capture dates are the part no one in the studio controls.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r10b5 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The Flickr account, and the photograph of the notice board. The studio's public Flickr account, under the display name Joe Chirum and the user name LivingArt8, was joined in March 2008 and holds some seven hundred photographs, most of them the studio's tie-dye and batik work and its paintings. It is a working photostream rather than a record of this project, and nothing in it should be read as evidence about the auction. One photograph in it does bear on this collection: the notice board published here as Art Notes, titled 'Art Notes' and marked 'Uploaded on June 2, 2013', at https://www.flickr.com/photos/24377024@N07/8921139312/ . The image is pixel-identical to the studio's held original. So the board, with the line 'WTF is Art ?!' on it, was publicly posted on a third party's platform three months before the museum letter of 2013-09-12, at an address anyone can check, on a date the platform assigned and the studio did not. It was not, on any evidence here, publicly read: that page records eight views in the thirteen years since. Flickr's 'Taken on June 1, 2013' on that photograph is not a capture date: it is the value Flickr supplies when an uploaded file carries no Exif, and the copy uploaded had been stripped. The studio's own original gives 2012-11-30, and the Art Notes record sets out how that date is bounded.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r10b6 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What else the photostream carries, enumerated on 2026-09-12, with its dates and its limits. Nine photographs in the account bear on this collection, and their upload dates fall in two groups. On 2013-06-02, in the same upload session as the Art Notes board, the account posted a digital work titled 'ArtHistoriansOTheFuture', at https://www.flickr.com/photos/24377024@N07/8920785186/ , which is the inscription of a studio board recoloured over a rainbow ground and whose words recur in the museum letter of 2013-09-12; the inscription-photograph record describes the board it was made from, photographed in 2011. The same session posted a painting titled 'WhatHedgeFunds(AcryliconCardboard)', at https://www.flickr.com/photos/24377024@N07/8921172858/ . That is a work carrying the title What Hedge Funds, publicly posted three months before the museum letter. The registrar's receipts record independently dates the registration of WHATHEDGEFUNDS.COM to 2011-10-16, and the 2014 capture described above shows the studio's own site publishing the line 'What Hedge Funds ?! invest in Art'. Three unrelated third parties - a photo host, a domain registrar and the Internet Archive - therefore fix three separate dates against that one title, and the studio set none of them. On 2014-05-18 the account posted six further works: 'ArtMarketReform7x10Watercolor' and 'ArtMarketReform9x12Acrylic', 'ArtTax7x10Watercolor1' and 'ArtTax7x10Watercolor2', and 'WhatHedgeFunds12x16Acrylic' and 'WhatHedgeFunds6x8Acrylic', with media and dimensions given in the titles and, on three of them, in a description field reading 'Acrylic on Canvas' or 'Mixed Media'. Art Market Reform and Art Tax are the two works the museum letter of 2013-09-12 names as Metaconceptual Art. These photographs postdate that letter by eight months, so they are consistent with it and are not evidence for it: what they establish is that the works the letter named exist as physical objects, not that they existed when it was written. Three of the 2014 photographs carry the public tag 'Metaconceptual'. That is the earliest publicly published use of the term located anywhere in this collection whose date is assigned by a third party - the 2013 letter is earlier but was private correspondence, and the profile biography that uses the term is undated and unarchived. One caution applies to all eight files: each shows a 'Taken on' value exactly one day before its upload date, which is Flickr's fallback for a file carrying no Exif. None of them carries a camera date, and the upload stamp is the only date any of them has.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r10b7 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The Flickr biography, and why it carries no date. The same account's profile biography states that in May 2009 the artist auctioned an artwork at a Sotheby's New York photograph auction, lot 16, 'as a performance act of metaconceptual Art', titled 'WTF is Ansel Adams ?!'. That is the designation this collection records, in the artist's own words, on a public page. It cannot be dated. A profile biography can be rewritten at any time and keeps no visible history, and the Internet Archive holds no capture of this profile at all, which was checked on 2026-09-11. It is therefore a public assertion of unknown date, and it repeats the same error as the 2013 letter in giving the sale as May 2009 when the catalogue gives 30 March 2009. Both are left as they stand, for the reason the museum correspondence record sets out.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r10b8 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What is not the studio's, and must not be cited as if it were. The domain wtfisart.com passed out of the studio's hands. A capture of 2016-07-11 shows a hosting suspension page, and from 2020 the domain carries an unrelated publication of art, film and book reviews written under several bylines, which is captured many hundreds of times between 2020 and 2025. The registry record was checked in 2026 and shows a fresh registration of 2024-08-29, so the domain lapsed and was taken up again by someone else. Nothing captured from 2016-07 onward is the studio's work, and the later site's contents say nothing about this collection. A separate incarnation captured on 2016-11-02, a wiki-style 'encyclopedic directory of Art' listing artists, is recorded here only so that a reader who finds it is not misled: it names no work of the studio's and makes no claim this collection relies on.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r10b9 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What this record does not show. None of these captures mentions the auction of 30 March 2009, lot 16, Ansel Adams, Sotheby's, or any performance act; the only public statement of that designation on these platforms is the undated Flickr biography. Nothing here bears on the 2007 origin asserted elsewhere in this collection. And the 2014 menu item 'Art Market Reform is a happening' is evidence that the studio used the word publicly of a work of its own on a date a third party fixed; it is not evidence of what the studio meant by the word in 2008, which the museum-approach record weighs separately.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r11b0 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence archive: the executed Sotheby's consignment agreement (March 2009)";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r11b1 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "record:evidence-consignment-agreement-2009";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r11b10 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The name of the file. The signed agreement was returned to Sotheby's on 2009-03-19 as an attachment called 'JoeArtChirum.pdf'. Read as the three-part personal name its form suggests, that is the consignor's given name, then the word Art where a middle name belongs, then his surname. His actual middle name, which appears on the identity page bound into this same file and is not reproduced here, is not Art. So the word has been substituted into his own name, deliberately, in the filename of the executed consignment contract, and sent under that name to the auction house. The covering message that carried the file is signed 'Joe M. Chirum', with his real middle initial. So in the same message the consignor signed himself by his ordinary name and named the attached contract with Art in that name's middle place; the substitution was made for the filename, not carried over from a habitual way of signing. This is a different kind of thing from the art-inflected phrases that run through the correspondence. Those are turns of phrase inside messages about appraisal, shipping and file formats. This is an act of self-naming, chosen rather than written in passing, and attached to the single most consequential document of the engagement at the moment of executing it. Alongside 'this happening' in the museum letter of 2008-08-11, it is the strongest contemporaneous indication in this collection that the person carrying out the consignment understood himself to be doing something artistic while doing it.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r11b11 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "How far the filename can be taken, and how far it cannot. It is evidence of self-conception, not of designation. A person may sign himself as an artist in the act of consigning a photograph without thereby making the consignment a work, and nothing in the file, the agreement, or the covering message says that the auction is a work. The reading also has an ordinary competitor: a jaunty filename from a correspondent who elsewhere signs off 'Yours in Art', chosen to be memorable rather than to mean anything. Against that competitor stands the placement, since the substitution falls exactly where a middle name falls and nowhere else, and the moment, since this is the contract rather than a covering note. The studio's own account, recorded in the correspondence record, is that the form of the work required the counterparty not to be told; a signature concealed in a filename is consistent with that account, and consistency is not proof of it. This record states the fact of the substitution and both readings, and chooses neither.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r11b12 crm:P14_carried_out_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    crm:P4_has_time-span _:b0_r11b13;
    a crm:E65_Creation;
    rdfs:label "Compilation of the consignment agreement record".
_:b0_r11b13 crm:P82a_begin_of_the_begin "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    crm:P82b_end_of_the_end "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    a crm:E52_Time-Span;
    rdfs:label "2026".
_:b0_r11b2 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Why this document is not published, first reason: it is contractually confidential. The agreement contains an undertaking by the consignor not to disclose its terms to any third party without Sotheby's prior written consent, excepting attorneys and accountants on a need-to-know basis and disclosure compelled by legal process. That undertaking binds the studio now. Accordingly this record states only what the document is, when it was made, who the parties were, and that it was signed by both of them. It gives no commission rate, fee, charge, settlement term, estimate, or reserve drawn from the agreement, and no account, consignment, or licence number appearing on its face. A reader should not infer that those terms are unremarkable or otherwise; the studio has simply not read them into the public record, and would need Sotheby's written consent to do so.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r11b3 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Why this document is not published, second reason: one page of it is a photocopy of a government-issued identity document, supplied for the identity verification the agreement requires. It carries a full legal name, date of birth, licence number, a residential address, physical description, and a photograph. That page will not be published in any form, redacted or otherwise, and the file as a whole is excluded from this repository by rule rather than by care, so that it cannot be committed by accident. The identifiers on it are held in the studio's local list of terms that the commit guard blocks.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r11b4 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The file as held, and what its metadata says. 'JoeArtChirum.pdf', 555,754 bytes, SHA-256 970c8e85bf0931ac8bfed1ab742d7f67247cdb2003c4db4aea99b9b29138eabe. Seven pages, none carrying a text layer: page one is a JPEG-compressed scan at 2548 by 3504 pixels, pages two to seven are bilevel CCITT Group 4 scans at 1274 by 1752. The document information dictionary gives a creator of Adobe Acrobat 7.08 and a producer of its Image Conversion Plug-in, which is what converts scanned images into a PDF. Its creation and modification dates are identical, D:20090319130724-07'00', that is 2009-03-19 at 13:07:24 with a stated offset of seven hours behind UTC. The individual page objects carry their own creation stamps of 13:05:43 and 13:05:44, ninety seconds before the document was assembled, which is the ordinary signature of pages scanned in one pass and then combined. The checksum is published so the held file can later be shown to be the one described here without the file itself being disclosed, following the convention used for the 2013 museum correspondence.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r11b5 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "An apparent discrepancy, recorded rather than smoothed over. The PDF states its creation as 13:07:24 at an offset of UTC minus seven, which is 20:07:24 UTC. The mail client displaying the message that carried it shows a send time of 12:16 in the afternoon of the same day. If that display were in Pacific daylight time, also UTC minus seven, the message would predate its own attachment by fifty-one minutes, which is impossible. The discrepancy is one of rendering, not of substance: a mail client shows times in the timezone configured for the account or the machine viewing it, not the timezone of the sender in 2009. Any setting of UTC minus eight or further west resolves it. The setting has since been observed rather than assumed. The same mailbox's client displays the museum letter of 2008-08-11 as sent at 3:01 PM, although that message's raw Date line reads 16:01:02 at UTC minus seven, which is 23:01 UTC; that is a rendering at UTC minus eight. At the same offset the 12:16 display here is 20:16 UTC, nine minutes after the file was made, which is the order the documents require. Both screenshots were taken in the same mail client, as the studio confirmed on 2026-09-10; that its timezone setting was unchanged between the two captures is assumed rather than shown. The display describes the viewer's settings, not the sender, so it says nothing about where the message was sent from. The point is noted because an auditor comparing the two figures will find it.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r11b6 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The message that carried it, and an artifact inside it. The Photographs department's message of 2009-03-18 is held as a screenshot. It states, in capitals, that it is imperative the agreement be signed and returned as soon as possible, and that a signed agreement is required before the photograph can be sold; the word 'agreement' is misspelled in the original. It speaks of 'the sale of your photograph on March 30, 2009', the auction house's own statement of the sale date in the message carrying the contract. It offers three ways to return it, 'by PDF in an email, by fax, or by FedEx or UPS', so the electronic return the studio made the following day was the first of the three the auction house itself proposed, and not an expedient of the studio's own. A second screenshot, of that return as it sits in the studio's sent mail, quotes the department's message beneath it with its raw header lines, giving its Date as 'Wed, 18 Mar 2009 18:36:10 -0400', which is 22:36:10 UTC; the signed file was assembled the next day at 20:07:24 UTC, in the order the exchange requires. The same view lists the attachment as 'JoeArtChirum.pdf' at 543 KB. The held file is 555,754 bytes, which is 542.7 kibibytes and displays as 543 KB. That agreement of name and size is consistent with the held file being the one attached; a displayed size is not a checksum, and it does not prove it. Below the department's signature the message carries a forwarded header from an internal address at the same institution, under the subject 'Message from KMBT_C352', which is the model designation of a Konica Minolta multifunction scanner: the agreement was scanned on the auction house's own device and mailed onward from it. That header displays a send time of 7:42 PM, recorded here as a rendering and not as a fact about New York, for the reason given above about displayed times. The studio's mailbox in 2009 was a Hotmail account, as the provider's promotional footer on the message shows, whereas the 2013 museum correspondence sits with a different provider; that bears on any future export of these messages as files with their headers intact.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r11b7 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What it settles. This collection recorded until now that the identity of the consignor of record was not established, because the published catalogue names no consignor. The consignment correspondence narrowed that; this closes it. A consignment agreement is the instrument by which a consignor consigns property for sale, and here it exists, is dated eleven days before the sale, names the studio principal, and is signed by him and countersigned for the auction house. Two limits still hold and are not affected. The catalogue itself remains silent, so a reader working only from public sources cannot see this; every statement elsewhere in this collection that the catalogue does not name Sun & Rain Works stays correct. The agreement is made in the studio principal's own name rather than the business name, which is what the business form requires and not a separation from it: the opening approach of 2008-08-14 told Sotheby's that its writer was 'the sole proprietor of a fledgeling Art business, Sun and Rain Works', and a sole proprietorship has no legal personality distinct from its proprietor, so it contracts in his name. On the ordinary understanding of that business form, the consignor named on this agreement and the business disclosed to the auction house at first contact are the same legal person. This record states that as the general position for a sole proprietorship rather than as advice on this contract, which is governed by New York law and has not been reviewed by a lawyer for this collection.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r11b8 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What it does not touch. The agreement is a contract for the sale of property at auction and contains nothing about art as a concept, a performance, a designation, or a project. The covering message that returned it carries two art-inflected phrases, a remark that an exception to saving paper is acceptable for the purposes of Art, and a sign-off reading 'Yours in Art'. Both are turns of phrase in a note about file formats. With this exchange the Sotheby's thread reaches eleven messages across seven months, from first approach to executed contract, and no message among them states that the consignment or the sale is an artwork. An earlier version of this passage gave the count as eight and said that nothing in the thread frames the consignment as an artwork. The first was stale and the second is withdrawn as too strong, for the reasons the correspondence record sets out: what is absent across the thread is a designation, not a vocabulary, the studio's own language in it being persistently art-inflected. That silence is not fatal to the designation this collection records, which is dated to 2013 and evidenced there, and the weight of that silence should not be overstated. Trade correspondence with a cataloguer and an administrator, about estimates, deadlines, shipping and an unreturned signature, is not where an artist would set out a conceptual framing, and the absence of one there is weak evidence about intention. It is worth more that the only letter of this period addressed to an art institution rather than to the trade, the approach to a museum photography department of 2008-08-11, is also the one place in the whole correspondence where a conceptual reading is available. What remains true, and is the actual gap, is that no document of 2008 or 2009 designates the auction as a work; the earliest that does is of 2013.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r11b9 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Redaction and consent. The Sotheby's staff member who sent and chased the agreement is identified by department and role only; the principal auctioneer named on the document's face is not named here either. Their names, the institutional telephone and mailbox details, the studio principal's addresses, and every account, consignment, licence and identity number appearing in the file are withheld and retained in the studio's private copy. Sotheby's holds its own executed counterpart of this agreement. Any publication of the document or of its terms would require that institution's written consent, which has not been sought at the date of this record.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r12b0 crm:P2_has_type <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/>;
    crm:P75i_is_possessed_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    a crm:E30_Right;
    rdfs:label "CC BY 4.0".
_:b0_r12b1 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence archive: documentation photographs of the Ansel Adams print (August 2008)";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r12b10 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "A correction to this record, about the date of that frame. An earlier version of this passage said the frame carried no reliable date, that a filesystem timestamp of 2009-01-20 was all that remained, and that no date was asserted for the series. That was wrong on every count, and it was written without consulting the record that already covers these files. The staged-photographs record dates the series to autumn 2008 by a three-part analysis which is stronger than anything offered here. Its floor is independent of any clock: the files were rewritten by QuickTime 7.5.5 running on Mac OS X 10.5.5, released on 2008-09-09 and 2008-09-15, so they cannot have been processed by that pairing before the middle of September 2008. Its middle term is the processing time those rewrites left, 2008-11-25 between 18:56:10 and 18:56:17 in frame order. And the 2009-01-20 timestamp cited in the earlier version of this passage is in that record too, correctly, as the ceiling rather than the date: it is the file system's record of the copy into the studio's working folder, not of the capture or the processing. That record also explains the file names, which this one did not: an Olympus that has never had its clock set reports 1 January, and builds 'P101' out of it. The date of capture itself remains unestablished, as it says. Nothing in that analysis is displaced by the copies from which the published frame was taken, whose own timestamps are a property of the copying and carry no weight.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r12b11 crm:P14_carried_out_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    crm:P4_has_time-span _:b0_r12b12;
    a crm:E65_Creation;
    rdfs:label "Compilation of the 2008 documentation-photograph evidence record".
_:b0_r12b12 crm:P82a_begin_of_the_begin "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    crm:P82b_end_of_the_end "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    a crm:E52_Time-Span;
    rdfs:label "2026".
_:b0_r12b2 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "record:evidence-consignment-photographs-2008";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r12b3 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence status: independently checkable. Unlike the studio's correspondence and receipts, this record can be tested by any third party without access to private material. The public Sotheby's catalogue entry for sale N08533, lot 16, at https://www.sothebys.com/en/auctions/ecatalogue/2009/photographs-n08533/lot.16.html describes the print as carrying the photographer's 'Photograph by Ansel Adams for The American Trust Company' stamp, as numbered 1-SF-46, as titled, and as annotated 'Wells Fargo Bank' in ink on the reverse by the photographer; the catalogue note explains the annotation as Adams's own correction following the 1960 merger of The American Trust Company into Wells Fargo Bank. Each of those particulars is visible in the studio's 2008 verso photograph, which is published with this record, except that the negative number is legible only as far as '1-SF-' before the right edge of the frame cuts it off; the catalogue's '46' is therefore not visible and is not claimed here. A vertical crease runs through the mount to the left of the number but does not obscure it. The correspondence is therefore in four particulars - the credit stamp, the negative-number series, the inked Wells Fargo annotation over the struck-through article, and the handwritten title - and is not dependent on the studio's own account.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r12b4 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Embedded image metadata, as recorded by the camera: all eleven files carry Exif written at capture by a FUJIFILM FinePix1300, reporting the firmware string 'Digital Camera FinePix1300 Ver1.24'. Exif DateTimeOriginal values run from 2008-08-08 00:05:33 to 2008-08-08 00:10:22, a continuous session of under five minutes. The frames are numbered DSCF0012 to DSCF0017 and DSCF0020 to DSCF0024; frames 0018 and 0019 are absent from the sequence. This metadata is written by the camera at exposure and is independent of the filesystem timestamps of the storage medium, which agree with it. It remains studio-held and is not notarized: no checksums, file hashes, or custody identifiers are published at this time, and Exif fields can in principle be altered.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r12b5 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Contemporaneous conceptual intent. One frame in the sequence, exposed at 2008-08-08 00:08:46, shows the print photographed together with a copy of 'The Philosophy of Andy Warhol (From A to B and Back Again)', stood upright and facing the camera on the same ground. Sun & Rain Works reads this juxtaposition as a deliberate art-referential gesture made at the time of documentation, and as the earliest dated trace of the thinking behind the concept of the auction as artwork. The scope of that reading should be stated plainly: the photograph evidences that the two objects were deliberately composed in one frame in August 2008. It is not a written designation, it does not by itself establish that the auction engagement was framed as art in 2008 or 2009, and the conceptual designation remains an assertion by the artist made explicit over time.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r12b6 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Limits of the correspondence. Ansel Adams produced multiple prints from the 1952 negative, so agreement on the verso markings identifies a print of the same issue and annotation state rather than establishing a unique object beyond question; the strength of the match rests on the combination of particulars, not on any one of them. The photographs show that the print was in the room in which they were taken; the attribution of that room and camera to Sun & Rain Works rests on the studio's custody of the storage medium. The Sotheby's catalogue names no consignor, as is standard auction practice, so the identity of the consignor of record is not established by these photographs. It is evidenced separately: the consignment correspondence record carries two messages of March 2009 in which the auction house's own Photographs department writes to the studio principal about 'your photograph' and chases the signed consignment agreement it had posted in February.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r12b7 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Corroborated from outside the files. The eleven frames described here were transmitted as a zip attachment to Sotheby's on 2008-08-14, six days after exposure, and that attachment is held: its twelve entries are byte-for-byte identical to the studio's loose copies, and its Info-ZIP UNIX extra fields carry Unix-epoch modification times of 2008-08-08 07:05:36Z to 07:10:24Z. The covering message describes the photographs as made with a 1.3 megapixel FujiFilm camera, which agrees with the FinePix1300 named in the Exif and with the 1280 by 960 frame size. The caveat recorded above, that Exif fields can in principle be altered, should now be read against that agreement. One frame in the set, DSCF0020, is the frame composing the print with Warhol's philosophy; it was among those sent. See the 2008 approach record.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r12b8 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The eleven frames are also held as loose files, and their two clocks agree. Besides the archive San Francisco.zip, the studio holds the eleven photographs as individual files carrying filesystem timestamps. Those timestamps run from 23:05:36 to 23:10:24 on 2008-08-07, while the EXIF DateTimeOriginal values of the same files run from 00:05:33 to 00:10:22 on 2008-08-08. The difference is constant, between fifty-nine minutes fifty-seven seconds and fifty-nine minutes fifty-eight seconds across the set, and the intervals between successive frames are the same in both series. This does not re-date the session, and an earlier reading of it that did has been discarded. Every filesystem timestamp falls on an even second, which is the two-second granularity of the FAT file system used by camera cards, and FAT stores local time with no timezone, so the same stored value is read an hour out when the reading system applies different daylight-saving rules. A one-hour offset between a FAT timestamp and an EXIF value is therefore the expected artefact of reading the card later and elsewhere, not evidence that either clock was wrong. What the agreement does establish is worth having on its own. The two series track each other frame for frame, so the session was a single unbroken run of about five minutes rather than an assembly from several occasions; and the files carry card timestamps at all, which means they were preserved from the camera card rather than re-saved by later software. The session date this collection uses remains the EXIF date, 2008-08-08, which is the camera's own record of its own exposure.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r12b9 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "A second photograph of the verso, from a different camera, showing the whole inscription. The close-up published with this record as images/adams-verso-2008.jpg is a frame from the session of 2008-08-08, and it is cropped: it carries the credit block and the inked 'Wells Fargo Bank' annotation, but its right edge cuts off the negative number after '1-SF-' and cuts the handwritten title off after 'San Francisco fr'. The studio also holds a photograph of the same verso made with a different camera, an Olympus C3100Z, in which the whole inscription is within the frame and legible: the printed block 'PHOTOGRAPH BY ANSEL ADAMS FOR THE AMERICAN TRUST COMPANY' with 'THE' struck through and 'Wells Fargo Bank' written in by hand, an empty 'NO.' line, 'NEGATIVE No. 1-SF-46', and beneath it, in the same hand, 'San Francisco from Twin Peaks'. It is published here as images/adams-verso-full-2008.jpg. Every element it shows is already independently attested by the public auction catalogue entry for lot 16 of sale N08533, which records the stamp, the number 1-SF-46, the title and the Wells Fargo annotation; the photograph adds legibility, not a new fact. That frame is not a stray file. It is one of a series of seventeen already recorded in this collection as the staged photographs of the print, where all seventeen are enumerated and the two verso frames are noted; what is new here is that one of them is published, so the whole inscription can be read rather than described.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r13b0 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence archive: the registrar's receipts for the studio's domains (2009-2016)";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r13b1 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "record:evidence-hosting-receipts-2009";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r13b10 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What the later invoices add, 2011 to 2016. Thirty of the forty-two invoices post-date the first year and fall under a second account number, the account having been renumbered between 2010 and 2011; the billing address moves from Hawai'i to Eugene, Oregon across the same gap. Three registrations among them bear on this collection. On 2011-10-16, on receipt 367713033, the account registered six .COM names in one bulk order at $9.59 each, among them WTFISART.COM, SUNANDRAINWORKS.COM and METACONCEPTUALART.COM, with privacy added to those and to others three days later on receipt 368276263. That is the earliest date this collection can place on METACONCEPTUALART.COM, which is the domain at which it is itself published. SUNANDRAINWORKS.ORG and SUNANDRAINWORKS.INFO follow on 2011-12-23, on receipt 385638142. ARTMARKETREFORM.ORG follows on 2012-11-21, on receipt 484082135, a year before the archived captures of art-market-reform material that the archived-websites record describes, with .NET and .INFO and four ARTMARKETBUBBLE names on 2013-05-23. Renewals run through the set: WTFISART.COM on 2012-10-17, 2015-01-04 and 2016-01-04; SUNANDRAINWORKS.ORG on 2013-01-05, 2014-12-22 and 2015-12-28; ARTMARKETREFORM.ORG on 2014-11-22 and 2015-11-23. The run ends at 2016-01-05, which is where the studio's held invoices stop rather than necessarily where the account's activity stops. Receipt 634164015 of 2013-12-27 also carries 'Domain Backorder' lines at $20.98 each against WTFISART.COM, SUNANDRAINWORKS.COM, WEGOTSOLDOUT.COM, WHATHEDGEFUNDS.COM and ARTMARKETREFORM.ORG, bundled with auction-membership renewals. A backorder is ordinarily a bid to capture a name when its current registration lapses, and these are names the account already held; what those lines mean on this account is not determined here. The same invoices register a number of further names unrelated to this collection, which are not itemised here.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r13b11 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The public registry contradicts these invoices, and the invoices are the better evidence. The domain registry was queried by RDAP on 2026-09-12, and it gives creation dates that do not match the studio's documents: SUNANDRAINWORKS.COM 2023-01-06, METACONCEPTUALART.COM 2023-01-27, WTFISART.COM 2024-08-29, and GROWINGALOHA.COM 2011-10-04. The invoices date the first three to a single bulk order of 2011-10-16 and the fourth to 2009-01-04. All four discrepancies run the same way, and the explanation is the one the wtfisart.com record already sets out for that domain: a registry creation date describes the registration object that currently exists, not the first time a registrant took the name. When a registration lapses the object is destroyed, and a later registration of the same string is a new object with a new date and a new handle. The original date is not retained anywhere in the public record and cannot be recovered from it. So a reader checking WHOIS today would conclude that the studio's own domain, and the domain at which this collection is published, are both two years old. They are not, and these invoices are what shows it. The consequence for anyone weighing this collection is worth stating in the open: on the question of when the studio first held these names, a dated invoice from the vendor is a business record and a registry creation date is not evidence at all, because the registry has no memory of what it has deleted. The consequence for the studio is less comfortable. Four registrations are now evidenced by held documents and by nothing else, so if the PDFs were lost there is no public source that would replace them. That is why the size and SHA-256 of all forty-two are published in the manifest beside this record, and why the originals should be preserved somewhere other than one machine.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r13b12 crm:P14_carried_out_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    crm:P4_has_time-span _:b0_r13b13;
    a crm:E65_Creation;
    rdfs:label "Compilation of the registrar's receipts record".
_:b0_r13b13 crm:P82a_begin_of_the_begin "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    crm:P82b_end_of_the_end "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    a crm:E52_Time-Span;
    rdfs:label "2026".
_:b0_r13b2 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence status: a third party's business record, and the firmest dating evidence in this collection for the website. The registrar generated these receipts, numbered them in its own sequence, and timestamped them in UTC; the studio could not set those dates, and the record can in principle be confirmed by the registrar from its own systems. It is held as the registrar's export, 4,931 bytes, SHA-256 0b4c8f9c660734765666279ff2c5a3f5a3f72219e3e9ff872d6e1e49ba8a1399, and as the matching PDF invoices, of which the three for 2009-03-16 are: the registration and blogcast purchase, 137,163 bytes, SHA-256 0bcec2aef8bd16e3ce2a60d02ab407d1ed76a7a15b86ca0270a1fd0ae6550cb0; its refund, 114,428 bytes, SHA-256 b963cd88dad2d6e5d90db67c55699ebb854e1180eb5d688c4840b9d4aa695d89; and the hosting purchase, 113,079 bytes, SHA-256 7cae6887d52c650ff7ac4998a67cc3b5bb51fcaf9e9266a5a6a97926c3bc772d. The documents themselves are not published: they carry the account holder's postal address, telephone number and mailbox, and the last four digits of a payment card. Checksums are published instead, as with the consignment agreement, so that the held files can later be shown unaltered without being disclosed. The size and SHA-256 of all forty-two invoices are published as a manifest beside this record, at data/evidence/registrar-receipts/manifest.json.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r13b3 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What this establishes, and it is narrow. The domain wtfisart.com was registered, and hosting for it was bought, on 2009-03-16, fourteen days before the auction of 30 March 2009 at which the studio consigned lot 16; and hosting on that account went on being paid for every month until at least 2009-12-15. Until now the date on which the studio first took the domain was undetermined from any public source, and the collection said so. It is now fixed by a document the studio did not write. Two qualifications belong with it. Only the first hosting purchase names wtfisart.com; the nine renewals name the product alone, so that they are renewals of that same account is an inference from the record's shape, reasonable but not stated in it. And one payment falls out of the monthly pattern, on 2009-07-25 rather than the fifteenth.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r13b4 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What it does not establish. It says nothing whatever about what was served at the domain. A registration is a name; hosting is rented space; neither is a published page, and no capture of the domain exists from that period. The WTFIA folder of source documents, worked on until the week before the sale, is what the studio assembled for the site, and the reconstruction record sets out that no HTML from the 2009 site survives anywhere. So the position is now: the domain and the hosting are documented from 2009-03-16, and what if anything stood at that address in 2009 remains unevidenced. This record also says nothing about the auction designation, and nothing about the 2007 origin asserted elsewhere in this collection.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r13b5 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The lapse, the parked page of 2011, and the re-registration that followed it. No renewal of WTFISART.COM appears among the invoices of the first year, which run to 2010-01-05 and do contain a renewal of the other domain. A registration taken on 2009-03-16 for one year would fall due on 2010-03-16. The Internet Archive's earliest capture of the domain, on 2011-02-10, shows a parked advertising page carrying no studio content, which is what a registrar commonly serves at an address whose registration has lapsed. That capture is therefore evidence about February 2011 and not about 2009, and the records of this collection which read it as positive evidence that the site was not live in 2009 have been narrowed to say what it can carry. The fuller set of invoices adds that the lapse was not permanent: on 2011-10-16, eight months after that capture, the account registered WTFISART.COM again, on receipt 367713033, in a bulk order of six .COM names at $9.59 each. An earlier version of this record said that no renewal of the domain appeared anywhere in it. That was true of the first year's invoices, which were all this record then held, and it is not true of the whole run.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r13b6 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The blogcast, bought and refunded in thirty-five minutes. In the same transaction as the domain registration, at 00:45:06 UTC on 2009-03-16, the account bought a registrar-hosted blogging product, 'Economy Quick Blogcast', for $9.98, and at 01:20:25 UTC it was refunded in full. The studio recalled an early blog of about this period which it has been unable to find. This purchase accounts for the recollection and, equally, explains why nothing has been found: the product was returned the same night, and nothing in this collection evidences a blog published at that time. The studio's only blog located on any platform is one opened in November 2014, whose owning profile records its own creation in that month.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r13b7 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The other domain, and a correction that is itself withdrawn. The same account registered GROWINGALOHA.COM on 2009-01-04, on receipt 146295715, and renewed it on 2010-01-05, on receipt 213590943. That is the sibling site whose Dreamweaver template and stylesheet the reconstruction copies, and those two purchases are documented by the registrar's own invoices. The public registry disagrees, and the paragraph below sets out why the invoices are the better evidence. This record previously carried a correction stating that the receipts do not name Sun and Rain Works, that the billing name on every line is the studio principal's own, and that the business is named nowhere in them. That correction was wrong and is withdrawn. It was drawn from the registrar's comma-separated export, which has no billing-name column at all, so the absence of a field was taken for the absence of a fact. The PDF invoices carry a 'Bill To' block, and on every one of the forty-two examined it gives the account holder's name, a postal address, and the line 'Sun and Rain Works'. That includes receipt 158832568, the registration of WTFISART.COM itself, and receipt 146295715, the registration of GROWINGALOHA.COM. The business is named by the registrar, in the registrar's own business records, on the first invoice of the run and on the last.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r13b8 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The name as the registrar recorded it. Receipt 158832568 records the string WTFISART.COM. A domain name carries no spaces and no punctuation, so the string does not settle how it is to be read: it reads as a question, 'WTF is art?', and equally as a statement, 'WTF is art', or 'WTF Is Art' taken as a title. Both readings are present in the string as the registrar printed it at 00:45:06 UTC on 2009-03-16; neither is supplied after the fact. The 'Bill To' block of the same invoice names Sun and Rain Works, so the name was taken by the business under which the studio works.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r13b9 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The studio's reading of the registration, given in 2026. The studio holds that the double reading was deliberate: that by choosing a name which is at once a question and a declaration it named the work and declared it art, and that the registration under the business's name puts that declaration on record. That is the studio's account of its intent, and this record keeps it apart from what the receipt shows. A registrar prints whatever string is bought, so the receipt fixes the date of the name and the business that took it, and records nothing of why the name was chosen. Nor does the business name mark this purchase out: the same 'Bill To' line appears on all forty-two invoices, including the registration of GROWINGALOHA.COM and names unrelated to this collection. What the receipt adds to the studio's account is independent dating. The words the studio now reads as the work's declaration were taken by the studio on 2009-03-16, fourteen days before the sale, on a document the studio did not write.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r14b0 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence archive: a photograph of two studio works inscribed 'W.T.F. is Art ?' (8 November 2011)";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r14b1 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "record:evidence-inscription-photograph-2011";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r14b10 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What this record does not show. It shows that the two works, and the inscription 'W.T.F. is Art ?', existed on or before 2011-11-08, by the file's own account. It does not date either work's making. It does not show that anyone outside the studio saw either physical work, or this photograph, at any date; the board's words reached a public platform in 2013 through the digital work, and the sheet's inscription is not shown to have reached one at all. It says nothing about what was served at wtfisart.com at any date, nothing about the 2009 auction designation, and nothing about the 2007 origin asserted elsewhere in this collection. It carries no statement that the phrase declares a work of art.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r14b11 crm:P14_carried_out_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    crm:P4_has_time-span _:b0_r14b12;
    a crm:E65_Creation;
    rdfs:label "Compilation of the inscription photograph record".
_:b0_r14b12 crm:P82a_begin_of_the_begin "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    crm:P82b_end_of_the_end "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    a crm:E52_Time-Span;
    rdfs:label "2026".
_:b0_r14b2 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence status: held by the studio; the file's own account, unusually complete. The file is the camera's original: a JPEG of 2,048 by 1,536 pixels with its Exif block intact, including the camera's 400 by 300 embedded thumbnail, which shows the same scene. It is held as 1108111218.JPEG, 487,576 bytes, SHA-256 469087b91ba1d18fb7beaa8f2d3bbec61959ea1ad57e9b1e2d3f645b29d73a00, and is published here at /images/wtf-is-art-sheet-2011.jpg as a byte-identical copy of that file, renamed for the web and not otherwise altered, so the checksum describes the published image as well. No person, face, vehicle plate or address is visible in it.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r14b3 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "How the photograph is dated, and how firmly. The Exif block names the camera as an LG Electronics LG-VX8575, a camera phone sold on the Verizon Wireless network, with firmware VX857V06, and gives DateTimeOriginal and DateTimeDigitized both as 2011:11:08 12:18:15. The field records no timezone and no location; the file carries no GPS data. The file name, 1108111218, is the phone's own naming, built from the month, day, two-digit year, hour and minute of capture, and it gives the same minute independently of the Exif field. The Software field names only the phone's firmware, and no editing application has written to the file. Two cautions go with this. Exif fields and file names can be rewritten, so the date remains the file's own account and is not notarized. And a phone of this kind ordinarily takes its clock from the carrier network rather than from its owner, which makes an accidental misdating less likely than with a camera whose clock is set by hand; it does not rule out a deliberate one. The file-system dates of the copy now held are of 2026 and say nothing about 2011.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r14b4 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The inscription, read closely. The words on the sheet run up its right-hand edge, rotated a quarter turn, in small dark capitals and lower case: an opening quotation mark, 'W.T.F.' with a full stop after each letter, 'is Art', a space, a question mark, and a closing quotation mark. They are legible when that part of the image is enlarged and turned upright; most of the surrounding notes on the sheet are not legible at this resolution and are not transcribed here. The inscription is punctuated as a question. That bears on the reading of the domain name set out in the hosting-receipts record, where the studio holds that wtfisart was chosen to read both as a question and as a statement. This photograph shows the studio writing the phrase as a question, in 2011; it is evidence that the question reading was the studio's own, and it is not evidence for the statement reading.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r14b5 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The board's phrase in the studio's letter of 2013. On 2013-09-12 the studio wrote to a university art museum, in the message the museum-correspondence record describes, that 'together we are helping the Art Historians of the future to keep their employment, here and now'. The board in this photograph carries the same words and the same closing 'here and now', and by the photograph's date it existed twenty-two months before that letter was sent. The letter is retained in the museum's own archive; the photograph is held by the studio. The agreement between them shows the phrase was the studio's own and in use before 2013. It does not date the board's making, which could be earlier than November 2011 but is not shown to be.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r14b6 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Where the photograph falls among the domain's dates. The registrar's receipts give WTFISART.COM as first registered on 2009-03-16, lapsed by 2011-02-10, when the Internet Archive recorded a parked page, and registered again on 2011-10-16. This photograph was taken twenty-three days after that re-registration. It shows the studio working with the phrase in its physical work at the time it took the name back. It is not evidence of anything in 2009, and it does not show the phrase in any public place in 2011.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r14b7 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The digital work made from the board, posted 2013-06-02. The studio's Flickr account, at flickr.com/photos/24377024@N07/8920785186/, carries a public photograph titled 'ArtHistoriansOTheFuture' with the description 'MetaConceptual Art'. Flickr records it as posted at 2013-06-02 05:55:56 UTC, which is the evening of 1 June 2013 on the American west coast. It is the board's inscription, cropped to the lettering and recoloured over a saturated rainbow ground: the same words, line breaks and letterforms as the board in the photograph of 2011, down to the tilde and closing parenthesis of '(Here and Now. ~)'. The studio states that the physical board was the foundation of this digital work. The studio's held copy, 8920785186_4cfd9c6af9_b.jpg, 1,022 by 285 pixels and 89,727 bytes, SHA-256 2e185a7bfa5e200aee5377ec2c7c523548e0de63d01aa27ac059463b7b82104f, was compared on 2026-09-15 with the file Flickr serves at that size and is byte-identical to it; Flickr also serves the image at up to 1,600 by 446 pixels. That held copy is published here at /images/art-historians-of-the-future-2013.jpg, renamed and otherwise unaltered, so the checksum describes the published image, the studio's held copy and the file Flickr serves alike. The file carries no Exif, and Flickr's 'taken' time of 2013-06-01 22:55:47, nine seconds before the posting, is the value it supplies for a file without one, as the archived-websites record sets out for the Art Notes photograph posted the same day. The posting date is assigned by the platform and is the evidence; the page recorded eleven views when checked. This places the board's words in public, on a third party's platform, three months before the museum letter. It does not date the digital work's making beyond that, and it does not carry the phrase W.T.F. is Art, which is on the sheet and not on the board.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r14b8 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Two works carry the line, a year apart. The sheet photographed here is the second work in this collection inscribed with the phrase. The first is the notice board published as Art Notes, which carries 'WTF is Art ?!' beside the name 'Sun and Rain Works', and which is photographed in a file dated 30 November 2012 and posted publicly to the studio's Flickr account on 2013-06-02, the same day as the digital work described above. This photograph is dated 2011-11-08, nearly a year earlier than the Art Notes file, and the two inscriptions are not the same object: they are written in different hands of the same writer, on different supports, and punctuated differently, 'W.T.F. is Art ?' here and 'WTF is Art ?!' there. The phrase therefore recurs across separate works of the studio rather than appearing once. What that carries is modest but real: the words were in continuous use in the studio's own work between the domain's first registration in 2009 and its public postings of 2013, and neither occurrence depends on the other for its date. Neither is a dated public act in itself; the Art Notes board reached a public platform in 2013, and this sheet is not shown to have reached one at all.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r14b9 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The titles, and the survival of the works, are the studio's account. Neither title, 'Helping the Art Historians of the future' or 'Dancing with Mummies', is written on either work in any part visible in the photograph; the board carries its phrase in quotation marks but not as a labelled title. The identification of the sheet's title, and its reference to Thomas Hoving, whose memoir of his directorship of the Metropolitan Museum of Art is titled Making the Mummies Dance, are given by the studio in 2026. The studio states that neither physical work survives. The sheet is therefore known only from this photograph, and the board from this photograph and the digital work made from it. When each physical work was made is not established by this record.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r15b0 crm:P2_has_type <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/>;
    crm:P75i_is_possessed_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    a crm:E30_Right;
    rdfs:label "CC BY 4.0".
_:b0_r15b1 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence archive: Sun & Rain Works auction engagement (2009)";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r15b2 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "record:evidence-live-auction-2009";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r15b3 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence status: mixed. Independently verifiable: (1) the public Sotheby's catalogue lot (sale N08533, lot 16) at https://www.sothebys.com/en/auctions/ecatalogue/2009/photographs-n08533/lot.16.html ; (2) two Sun and Rain Works public posts carrying the 'WTF is Art' theme, both still live on the account when checked on 2026-09-10, and each dated independently by the platform's timestamp-encoded identifier. The earlier reads '#WTF is #Art ?!?!?!' (X post 372900008367894529). Its identifier decodes to 2013-08-29T01:54:24Z, and the platform's own embed gives August 29, 2013. The studio's screenshot of the post shows Aug 28, 2013, because a displayed date is rendered in the viewer's timezone, and 01:54 UTC on 29 August is still 28 August in every timezone from UTC minus two westward, which includes all of the Americas. The later reads '#WTF is #Art ?!' (X post 373257603108704256). Its identifier decodes to 2013-08-30T01:35:22Z, and the embed gives August 30, 2013. The two were posted 23 hours and 41 minutes apart. Neither is assigned a local day here: nothing in this collection places the studio in a particular timezone in 2013. An earlier version of this passage dated the second post to 2013-08-29 by taking Hawaii-Aleutian time as the studio's local time; that assumption is withdrawn. Held privately by the studio: the 2008-2009 correspondence, receipts, and photographs. Selected private items can be promoted to public evidence as redaction and rights review are completed.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r15b4 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The consignment engagement is corroborated by inbound 2008 correspondence from Sotheby's Photographs (confirming intake logistics and naming the department administrator) and by a shared Hana, HI mailing address linking the consignor to the Sun & Rain Works domain receipts. Contemporaneous conceptual intent is evidenced by art-referential documentation (an Andy Warhol volume juxtaposed with the photograph in images sent to Sotheby's). Each item is scoped to what it shows: these evidence identity, consignment, intent, and the concept's public continuity — not a witnessed live-creation event, and not precedence over any later artist or event.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r15b5 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Part of the documentation described here has since been dated and published as a record in its own right. The art-referential documentation photographs — including the frame composing the print with a volume of Warhol's philosophy — carry camera-written Exif placing them on 2008-08-08, and their close-up of the print's verso agrees in four particulars with the public Sotheby's catalogue entry for lot 16. That material is no longer only studio-reported: see the documentation-photograph evidence record.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r15b6 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The catalogue entry, captured and checked, and a gap that remains open. This collection calls the public catalogue entry for lot 16 its independently verifiable anchor, and thirteen records rely on it - for the sale date, the lot and title, the 1952 dating, the verso particulars against which the documentation photographs are tested, the estimate, and five times over for what it does not say, which is that it does not name Sun and Rain Works. Until now it was held as a live URL and nothing else. It has been fetched, and what it said on the date of fetching is now fixed: the page, its byte count and its SHA-256 are recorded in a manifest under data/evidence/, together with a transcription of the particulars the records depend on. The page itself is third-party commercial content and is not republished. Three things were confirmed at capture. The verso description matches the studio's photographs, including the frame in which the whole inscription is legible. The estimate of 7,000 to 10,000 US dollars matches the figure the auction house gave in its message of 2008-08-15, so that figure now has two independent sources. And the entry names no consignor, so the negative statement this collection repeats is true of the page as fetched. One detail is new: the catalogue attributes the 'Wells Fargo Bank' annotation to Adams himself. The gap is now closed, and how it closed is worth recording. When this passage was first written the Internet Archive held no capture of lot 16 in any form, while the catalogue around it was archived:  The same sale has been crawled from 2016 to 2025, ten of its lots have some capture and seven have a successful one, among them lots 27, 44, 61, 73 and 78; the locale-qualified form of these URLs is crawled too, lot 27 having been taken twice in that form. So the archive reaches this catalogue and has done for nine years. Lot 16 was a hole in covered ground rather than an unarchived site, and it was a small thing to fix. It was fixed on 2026-09-21, when the page was captured from a logged-in archive.org account at 02:28:20 UTC, together with a locale-qualified form of the same address a few minutes later which carries the same content digest. The snapshot has been fetched and read: it carries the lot number, the title, the American Trust Company stamp, the number 1-SF-46, the Wells Fargo annotation, the 1952 dating and the estimate of 7,000 to 10,000 US dollars, which is every particular the records here depend on. So the anchor no longer rests on a commercial auction house keeping a 2009 page online: a copy is held by a third party with no stake in this collection, and it can be cited beside the live address. One detail of the earlier attempt is kept rather than tidied away. An anonymous save was requested on 2026-09-20 and refused, because anonymous archiving now requires an account; that is why the page went unarchived while its neighbours did not, and why the capture had to be made by someone signed in. The publisher was never the obstacle - the site's robots.txt disallows only the lot PDF and carries no archiver exclusion. The studio's own fetch of 2026-09-20 is still worth having, for a different reason than before: it fixes what the page said a day before the archive's copy was taken, so the two can be compared. A note on the address, since two forms of it circulate. The page answers both at the bare lot path and with a locale=en query appended, and on the capture date the two served byte-identical content, the same 42,070 bytes under the same SHA-256. Either may be cited and the checksum recorded here covers both.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r15b7 crm:P14_carried_out_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    crm:P4_has_time-span _:b0_r15b8;
    a crm:E65_Creation;
    rdfs:label "Compilation of the auction-engagement evidence record".
_:b0_r15b8 crm:P82a_begin_of_the_begin "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    crm:P82b_end_of_the_end "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    a crm:E52_Time-Span;
    rdfs:label "2026".
_:b0_r16b0 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence archive: a photograph of a shop sign reading META, Italy (27 February 2012)";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r16b1 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "record:evidence-meta-sign-2012";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r16b2 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence status: held by the studio; the camera's original file, with its Exif intact. The file is held as 'March2012 382.JPEG', 1,542,919 bytes, SHA-256 7cbd3cab10fed318ab248b0c897dfbc8b3bb2c010c5925444e5a94c1b675277a. It is 4,000 by 3,000 pixels, the full frame size of the camera it names, a Canon PowerShot ELPH 100 HS, and the Exif dimension fields agree with the image. The camera's own thumbnail is embedded and shows the same frame. The exposure fields are those of a hand-held night photograph: 1/50 second at f/5.9, ISO 800, flash not fired, focal length 20mm. No editing application has written to the file, and no software field beyond the camera's own is present. The file carries no GPS data. The image shows no person and no private premises; the sign is a business's own public frontage. It is published here at /images/meta-sign-2012.jpg, a byte-identical copy of the held file, renamed for the web and not otherwise altered, so the checksum describes the published image as well.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r16b3 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The file name says March and the camera says February. The held file is named 'March2012 382.JPEG', while all three date fields in the Exif read 2012-02-27. A name of that form is what an import folder gives a batch of photographs, so the likeliest reading is that the file was copied off the camera in March 2012 and took the name of that batch, some days after the frame was taken. The discrepancy is stated here rather than resolved: on the file's own account the capture was 27 February 2012, and the name is evidence of when it was filed, not of when it was made. As with every camera file in this collection, the Exif date is the file's own account and is not notarized.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r16b4 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What the photograph shows, and what it does not say about the word. The sign is a retail trade name. META with a grave accent, written without the accent in this record's title for technical reasons, is the name of a chain of Italian stores, and the word in Italian means half; the English prefix meta, from the Greek, is a different word that the Italian trade name does not carry. Nothing in the photograph connects the business to the studio, and no claim of any connection is made: the business is not a party to this collection, its sign is recorded here because the studio photographed it, and the record asserts nothing about the company. Nor does the photograph have anything to do with any later corporation that has taken the name Meta. What the image itself shows about the photographer is only this: the frame is filled by the sign at night, deliberately composed and tightly cropped, so the sign was the subject rather than an incidental part of a street view.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r16b5 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The studio's account of why it was taken. The studio states that the photograph was not incidental: that it saw the word meta on a shop front in Italy, five years after it had named Metaconceptual Art, and photographed it for that reason, as an instance of its own term standing in the ordinary commercial world. That is an account of intent, given in 2026, and no document of 2012 records it. The photograph's evidential value is narrow and should be stated plainly: it dates a frame to 2012-02-27 by the file's account, and it shows what was framed.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r16b6 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What this record does not show. It does not establish where the photograph was taken: the file carries no GPS, the Italian wording on the sign is consistent with Italy but is not proof of it, and the location is the studio's account. It records no work of art made by the studio, no public act, and no statement by the studio at the time. It does not evidence the 2007 naming of Metaconceptual Art, which is asserted elsewhere in this collection and remains unevidenced by any document of that year. It says nothing about the domain wtfisart.com, the 2009 auction designation, or the museum letter of 2013, and it establishes no priority of any kind over any other use of the word.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r16b7 crm:P14_carried_out_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    crm:P4_has_time-span _:b0_r16b8;
    a crm:E65_Creation;
    rdfs:label "Compilation of the META sign photograph record".
_:b0_r16b8 crm:P82a_begin_of_the_begin "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    crm:P82b_end_of_the_end "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    a crm:E52_Time-Span;
    rdfs:label "2026".
_:b0_r17b0 crm:P2_has_type <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/>;
    crm:P75i_is_possessed_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    a crm:E30_Right;
    rdfs:label "CC BY 4.0".
_:b0_r17b1 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence archive: correspondence declaring Metaconceptual Art to a university art museum (2013)";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r17b10 crm:P82a_begin_of_the_begin "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    crm:P82b_end_of_the_end "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    a crm:E52_Time-Span;
    rdfs:label "2026".
_:b0_r17b2 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "record:evidence-museum-correspondence-2013";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r17b3 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence status: retained by the receiving institution and produced by it on request, published here in redacted form, with machine-generated headers that corroborate the date internally. The message carries five timestamps written by two unrelated mail systems, in monotonic order. Four are Yahoo message identifiers whose leading integers are server-written Unix timestamps: 1379027969, 1379028560, 1379028776 and 1379028931, decoding to 2013-09-12 at 23:19:29, 23:29:20, 23:32:56 and 23:35:31 UTC - 16:19 to 16:35 Pacific, the studio's local time, spanning sixteen minutes of one outbound thread. The fifth is the recipient Exchange server's own stamp on the reply, 'Thu, 12 Sep 2013 23:38:09 +0000', two and a half minutes after the last of them; the quoted 'Sent' line reads 4:36 PM, agreeing to within a minute. Yahoo generated those identifiers and the University of Oregon's server copied them verbatim into its References and In-Reply-To headers, so each system corroborates the other. The dating therefore does not rest on the studio's account: it would require consistent, ordered forgery across two independent providers.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r17b4 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Publication and custody. This was private correspondence, not something its author published, so the message is not reproduced here in full. A redacted copy is published at data/evidence/museum-correspondence-2013/, withholding the replying staff member's name and both mailbox addresses while retaining the email domains, every header bearing on the date, the full text of the studio principal's declaration, and the substance of the museum's reply. The unredacted original is held by the studio on separate media, and its SHA-256 is published in that manifest so the held file can be shown to be unaltered without being disclosed; it can be released, in whole or in part, if the third party consents. Custody, corrected. An earlier version of this record stated that the sending mailbox had been closed and that the studio's saved file was therefore the only copy known to exist. That was wrong, and wrong in this project's favour. The file described in the manifest as the unredacted original is not a copy saved from the sending mailbox in 2013: it is the copy the receiving museum retrieved from its own archive and supplied in July 2026, and the two are the same file. Its headers settle the provenance. It carries no Received headers and none of the sending provider's X-headers, which a mailbox-saved copy would have; it carries four X-MS-Exchange-Organization headers, including the originating client and server addresses, which exist only inside the university's own mail organisation, and the originating client address falls inside UONET, 128.223.0.0/16, the University of Oregon's allocation in the ARIN registry. The message is therefore not studio-held evidence. The institution named in it retained it for close to thirteen years and produced it on request, and because the museum's reply quotes the studio principal's letter in full, the university's own archive carries the text of the declaration.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r17b5 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What the correspondence does and does not establish. It establishes that the declaration existed in writing on 2013-09-12 and was delivered to a named art institution, which is the earliest such delivery the studio has produced. It does not establish that the movement originated in 2007, and it is not itself evidence of anything before its own date; the 2007 origin remains asserted by the studio. The 'May 2009' dating of the auction in this letter is incorrect. Sotheby's own record gives sale N08533, Photographs, as 30 March 2009 in New York, 169 lots. The letter's month is therefore the artist's misrecollection, written four years after the event and before the catalogue record had been consulted. The 2008 consignment correspondence supplies part of the explanation: throughout that thread Sotheby's described the sale as its April 2009 sale, and the consignment was arranged against an April date. The sale actually took place on 30 March. A recollection of late spring rather than March is what an artist working from the plan of record would be left with, which makes the error the ordinary kind rather than a careless one. It remains an error; the letter says May, and no document says May. The error is confined to the month: the sale number, the lot number, the photographer, and the title given in the letter all match the catalogue entry. The same month appears in the artist's public Flickr profile, written in the same state of knowledge. Sun & Rain Works has elected to leave that profile unaltered, as an artifact of what was believed at the time rather than a statement to be brought into line with the record; the two texts are consequently left disagreeing with the catalogue and agreeing with each other. Neither is corrected here. An uncorrected error carried across two independent self-descriptions is itself evidence of the kind of account it is - remembered rather than constructed - and revising it after the fact would remove that. His statement that eight defining characteristics were already publicly findable in September 2013 is likewise his own, and the material he referred to has not been located.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r17b6 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The museum's role is receipt, and nothing more. The reply acknowledged the message and said it would be forwarded to staff. It records no exhibition, acquisition, review, endorsement, or acceptance of the work or of the term, and none should be inferred from this record: the Jordan Schnitzer Museum of Art is named here because the identity of the receiving institution is what gives the delivery its evidential weight, not because it participated in the work. Following this corpus's convention, the individual member of staff who replied is identified by role only; their name and the correspondents' email addresses are withheld from this public record and retained in the studio's private copy. The saved thread preserves the text parts only: the letter refers to attached images, which are not present in the file held.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r17b7 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "A negative finding, recorded because it bears on a claim this collection makes elsewhere. On 2026-07-11 the studio asked the museum whether its email archive reached back to March 2009, saying that Sun & Rain Works had sent it a press release around the Sotheby's Photographs sale of 30 March 2009. On 2026-07-15 the museum's communications manager replied that the museum holds no email from the studio's then-current address dated 2009, and attached the 2013 thread instead. No institutional corroboration for a 2009 approach to this museum therefore exists. The finding should be read for exactly what it is: a search that returned nothing, not a demonstration that nothing was sent. A 2009 message could have been addressed to staff whose mail was not searched, or have fallen outside a retention window, or have predated the present holder's tenure. What can be said is that the museum's holdings do reach 2013, that they were searched on request, and that nothing from 2009 came back. The studio's account of a 2009 press release to this institution remains unsupported by the recipient's own records.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r17b8 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What the letter names, and what it does not. The letter is the only document in this collection in which the studio sets out its titled works to an institution. It names two: 'Two popular examples of Metaconceptual Art, which are ongoing through time and space as well, are titled \"Art Market Reform\" , and \"Art Tax\"', and glosses the first as promoting accountability, transparency and regulation in the art market. It names its business, 'Sun and Rain Works', and gives the writer's role there as 'Chief Executive Artist'. It reports that a proposal for both works went to the White House two days earlier, which this collection has not verified and does not assert. And it mentions the 2009 sale, in the same letter, without giving it a title, calling it a performance act of Metaconceptual Art. The naming is the earliest dated use of either title held here, and the absence of a title for the auction is recorded in the concept record as evidence rather than as silence.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r17b9 crm:P14_carried_out_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    crm:P4_has_time-span _:b0_r17b10;
    a crm:E65_Creation;
    rdfs:label "Compilation of the 2013 museum-correspondence evidence record".
_:b0_r18b0 crm:P2_has_type <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/>;
    crm:P75i_is_possessed_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    a crm:E30_Right;
    rdfs:label "CC BY 4.0".
_:b0_r18b1 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence archive: public postings of Metaconceptual Art (2011-2012)";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r18b10 crm:P14_carried_out_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    crm:P4_has_time-span _:b0_r18b11;
    a crm:E65_Creation;
    rdfs:label "Compilation of the 2011-2012 public-posting evidence record".
_:b0_r18b11 crm:P82a_begin_of_the_begin "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    crm:P82b_end_of_the_end "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    a crm:E52_Time-Span;
    rdfs:label "2026".
_:b0_r18b2 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "record:evidence-pastebin-2011-2012";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r18b3 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence status: publicly verifiable on a third-party platform, and the earliest such evidence in this collection. The postings are readable by anyone at https://pastebin.com/AmQ3P87v and https://pastebin.com/18ndp2CF ; both are set never to expire. Their dates are assigned by the platform at the moment of posting and are carried in the page markup as 'Thursday 22nd of November 2012 11:19:57 PM CDT' and 'Monday 28th of November 2011 07:51:51 AM CDT'. Unlike a profile biography, the body of a paste is fixed once posted. The platform also records public circulation: 950 views for the 2012 posting and 433 for the 2011 one at the time of compilation. Nothing here is studio-held, so verification requires no access to the private archive.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r18b4 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Relation to the published Eight Sentences. The 2012 posting is the earliest located instance of the sentence sequence and predates its publication on this site by fourteen years. Its wording is the wording published here, allowing for three trivial variances: 'commodification of Art, and its relationship' has since lost its comma, 'the Art World , and Art History' has lost a space, and 'Transcendance' has been corrected to 'Transcendence'. The persistence of a misspelling across the interval, and its later silent normalisation, are recorded because they are the sort of detail by which a text's descent is traced. The sequence's stated debt to Sol LeWitt's 'Sentences on Conceptual Art' is not present in the 2012 posting and appears to be a later framing.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r18b5 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Limits. Both postings are the artist's own; the platform dates when he said something publicly, not whether what he said was true. They therefore evidence the public existence of the term and its definition at those dates, and nothing about the 2009 auction, which neither posting mentions. The 2011 posting describes its poem as written 'a few years ago', which would place composition somewhere in the late 2000s; that is the artist's own statement, made in 2011 rather than in retrospect from the present, and it narrows but does not close the gap to the asserted 2007 origin. The origin date itself remains unevidenced. Both postings link to a Sun & Rain Works page on Facebook. That page has since been deleted by the studio, and the Internet Archive holds no capture of it, so it cannot be examined and any earlier material it carried is lost. Its former existence is not in doubt: the 2012 posting cites its address and presents the eight characteristics as the page's content, which places the definition on that page on or before 2012-11-22. The postings recorded here are consequently the surviving witness to a source that no longer exists, and they are load-bearing for that reason.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r18b6 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Preservation note. Of the sources that once carried this material, several no longer exist: the Facebook page has been deleted, the YouTube videos cited in the 2013 correspondence are gone, and the mailbox from which that correspondence was sent has been closed. What survives does so on a third-party platform the studio does not control, in two postings set never to expire. Their continued availability is not guaranteed by anyone. The studio has therefore taken a local copy of both postings - the paste bodies and the rendered pages, the latter retained because they carry the platform's own timestamp string that the raw endpoint omits - published alongside this record with checksums at data/evidence/pastebin-2011-2012/. Those files are a preservation copy and not the dated source: a local file proves what was retrieved and when it was retrieved, and nothing more. The dating rests on Pastebin's records, which assign a posting timestamp the poster cannot alter, and should be verified against the live URLs for as long as they resolve.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r18b7 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Context for November 2011, independently dated. The studio states that in the autumn of 2011 it set up and ran two wiki sites for the Occupy movement, at wikioccupy.org and wikioccupy.com. Part of that is verifiable without reference to the studio: the Internet Archive holds a capture of wikioccupy.org made on 2011-12-03 at 04:10:07 UTC returning a status of 200, which is to say the site was live and serving content at that date. It is the earliest capture the Archive holds for the domain. No capture of wikioccupy.com exists, and both domains have since lapsed and are now unregistered. The contents of the archived page have not yet been read; the Archive was rate-limiting the machine used for this check.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r18b8 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What that context does and does not do. It does not evidence Metaconceptual Art. Running a wiki for a protest movement is not making art, and nothing on the archived site has been shown to concern the studio's work. Its relevance is to reading, not to dating: the posting of 'The Art World is Flat' on 2011-11-28 was made in the same weeks that its author was, on independent evidence, operating web infrastructure for a movement whose subject was finance and inequality. A poem about the flatness of the art world, published in honour of Cyber Monday by someone then running an Occupy wiki, is more legible for knowing that. Two smaller points also follow. The studio's account of the wiki is a claim it did not control the checking of, and it checks out, which is a modest reason to take its other accounts of that period seriously. And a person who registered and ran domains for projects in 2011 is the kind of person who might have done so for www.wtfisart.com in 2009, which makes that claim more plausible in kind without dating it by a single day.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r18b9 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "An outstanding lead, recorded so it is not lost. The studio holds three chat logs downloaded from meetings of that period, sent to a correspondent on 2012-11-12 as attachments named for their dates: two of 2011-11-10 and 2011-11-19, and a third of 2011-11-19. Those dates fall eighteen and nine days before the posting of 2011-11-28, which is presently the earliest located use of the term this collection records. The logs have not been examined. If the term, the eight characteristics, or the concept in recognisable form appears in them, the earliest evidence in this collection moves earlier, and it moves into a different and better kind of source: conversation with other people, rather than the studio's own solo posting. If it does not appear, that is a negative worth recording with the same care.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r19b0 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence archive: a painted postcard inscribed 'For the Love of Art' and signed 2008 (photographed 2011)";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r19b1 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "record:evidence-postcard-work-2008";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r19b2 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence status: held by the studio; a derived copy, not the camera's original file. The file is held as ArtFlowers(AcrylicandInkonPostcard).JPEG, 338,239 bytes, SHA-256 e58a9d0872a28f05b3758bede1302af64051f8facb4bb772a57c95347296a7ac. Its Exif names the camera as an LG Electronics LG-VX8575 with firmware VX857V06, the same phone and firmware as the photograph of 2011-11-08 described in the inscription-photograph record, and gives DateTimeOriginal and DateTimeDigitized as 2011:11:16 07:29:20. Three things show that the held file is not what the camera wrote. Its dimensions are 1,600 by 1,217 pixels, where that phone writes 2,048 by 1,536, and the proportions are not the camera's, so the image has been cropped; the Exif dimension fields were rewritten to match, which the cropping software did; and the camera's embedded thumbnail is gone. The file name is descriptive rather than the phone's own date-built name, so it was also renamed. The capture fields survived that processing, but nothing in the file corroborates them, as the photograph of 2011-11-08 is corroborated by its own file name and thumbnail. The date is therefore the file's account, at one remove. The photograph is published here at /images/for-the-love-of-art-postcard-2008.jpg, a byte-identical copy of the held file, renamed for the web and not otherwise altered, so the checksum describes the published image as well. No person, face, address or third party's private matter is visible in it.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r19b3 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What is written on the work, and what is printed under it. The handwritten text is legible in full: 'For the Love of Art, these tulips are beautiful! Almost as good as in real life', in quotation marks, then 'J.M.C.' and '08' at the right, then 'P.S. \"Double the Fun\"'. The signature on the painted side reads 'Joe Chirum' above '08'. The postcard's own printed caption is only partly legible at this resolution: the artist's name Cezanne with dates, a title naming tulips, a canvas measurement, a named memorial collection, and a copyright line for an art museum in Chicago; a printed line along the inner edge reads 'Great Impressionist Painting Postcards' with that museum's copyright. The postcard series' year of publication is not legible, and no attempt is made here to supply the missing words from a catalogue. The reproduction is a third party's published postcard, and the museum's copyright line appears on it. The painting reproduced is by an artist who died in 1906 and is itself long out of copyright; the postcard appears in this photograph as the support of the studio's own work, which is painted and written over it. The museum's notice is recorded here rather than dismissed, and the photograph is published on that footing.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r19b4 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The date of the work is the work's own statement. '08' is written twice on the object, once beside the initials on the inscription and once under the signature. That is the artist's own dating, made on the work, and it is not independent of the artist. No document in this collection dates the postcard's making, and the photograph that records it was taken three years later. So the position is: the work says 2008, and the earliest date any record here can fix for it is 2011-11-16. If the postcard series can be identified and its publication year read, that would give a floor below which the work cannot have been made, and that is worth doing.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r19b5 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What the inscription does and does not join. 'For the Love of Art' is a phrase about art written on a work, as 'W.T.F. is Art ?' is on the sheet photographed eight days earlier and 'WTF is Art ?!' is on the Art Notes board. The three are not the same phrase and this record does not treat them as one: what they share is the practice of writing a line about art onto the work itself, which is visible across the studio's objects from at least 2011 by the files' own dates. The postcard adds one thing the others do not. It is made on a museum's reproduction of a famous painting, painted over and then addressed back to art, which is the same gesture the collection describes elsewhere as working on the institution's own material. Whether the studio intended that reading in 2008 is the studio's account and is not recorded in any document of the time.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r19b6 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What this record does not show. It does not date the making of the work; it fixes only that the work existed, framed, by 2011-11-16, on the file's own account. It does not show that anyone outside the studio ever saw the work, and no public posting of it has been found at the date of this record. It says nothing about the domain wtfisart.com, nothing about the 2009 auction designation, and nothing about the asserted 2007 origin of Metaconceptual Art. Whether the physical work survives is not established here.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r19b7 crm:P14_carried_out_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    crm:P4_has_time-span _:b0_r19b8;
    a crm:E65_Creation;
    rdfs:label "Compilation of the painted postcard record".
_:b0_r19b8 crm:P82a_begin_of_the_begin "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    crm:P82b_end_of_the_end "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    a crm:E52_Time-Span;
    rdfs:label "2026".
_:b0_r1b0 crm:P14_carried_out_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    crm:P4_has_time-span _:b0_r1b1;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by _:b0_r1b2;
    a crm:E12_Production;
    rdfs:label "Making of the Art Notes board".
_:b0_r1b1 crm:P82a_begin_of_the_begin "2010-05-20T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    crm:P82b_end_of_the_end "2012-11-30T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    a crm:E52_Time-Span;
    rdfs:label "between 20 May 2010 and 30 November 2012".
_:b0_r1b10 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Publicly posted from 2013-06-02. The same photograph is public on the studio's Flickr account, under the display name Joe Chirum and the user name LivingArt8, titled 'Art Notes' and marked 'Uploaded on June 2, 2013', at https://www.flickr.com/photos/24377024@N07/8921139312/ . The file there is pixel-identical to the studio's held original. So the board, and the line 'WTF is Art ?!' on it, stood at a publicly readable address on a third party's platform three months before the museum letter of 2013-09-12, with an upload date assigned by the platform rather than by the studio. That is the whole of the claim. The page records eight views in the thirteen years since, so nothing here says the line was read; only that it was posted, and dated by someone other than its author. Flickr's 'Taken on June 1, 2013' on that page is not a capture date: it is the value Flickr supplies when an uploaded file carries no Exif, and the copy uploaded had been stripped of it. The dating of the photograph itself is unchanged and is set out elsewhere in this record. The archived-websites record describes that account and the rest of the platform evidence.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r1b11 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Dating, and what carries it. The board is evidenced by a single photograph held by the studio. That file's Exif, written by the camera, gives DateTimeOriginal, DateTimeDigitized and DateTime all as 2012:11:30 13:25:01, with no time zone recorded, and identifies the camera as a Canon PowerShot ELPH 100 HS, firmware 1.01, at f/2.8, 1/20s, ISO 250, 5.0mm, flash fired. The Canon MakerNote block survives intact at 3,640 bytes and its ImageType string reads 'IMG:PowerShot ELPH 100 HS JPEG'. The file carries no editing-software provenance: its only XMP is a single Windows-written xmp:Rating of 0, and there is no CreatorTool or ModifyDate. Two independent floors bracket the date from below and do not depend on the camera clock, which a user can set at will. First, the content of the board: the Paris museum theft the pinned New York Times report describes took place on the night of 19-20 May 2010, so the board as photographed cannot have been assembled before 20 May 2010, and the Christie's sale the other cutting reports took place on 4 May 2010. Second, the camera: the PowerShot ELPH 100 HS was announced on 7 February 2011 and shipped that March, so no file could have been made with it before 2011. The camera-written date of 30 November 2012 is consistent with both floors and is contradicted by nothing in the file. It remains a self-reported date on a studio-held file, of the same evidential kind as the rest of the private archive and weaker than the platform-assigned dates of the 2011-2012 postings; it is not independently notarized.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r1b12 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Custody and publication. The photograph came to light in the studio's private source archive, on the same medium as the WTFIA folder described in the 2008-2009 evidence record; it is not one of the ninety-seven files that record enumerates, and it is later in date than all of them. The original as held is 4,000 x 3,000 pixels, 1,056,962 bytes, SHA-256 29c40767175e790b7ae443d3e020d27c13f645909811c59c95640721dd165081. What is published here is a display derivative, resampled to 1,600 x 1,200, 345,612 bytes, SHA-256 220b145dfa5464293edb91d0bbd5d2a1d9edc9bd99ab58f0768b66d0194bd320, carrying the camera's Exif block forward unaltered, including the MakerNote. The reduction is deliberate and is a rights measure, not a conservation one: at full resolution the body text of the two pinned press cuttings is readable, and at this size it is not, while the handwritten panel that is the work remains fully legible. The checksum of the original is published so the held file can later be shown to be the one described here without the full-resolution image being released.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r1b13 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Scope. This record establishes that a board bearing the line 'WTF is Art ?!' and the name 'Sun and Rain Works' stood in the studio and was photographed on a date the file gives as 30 November 2012, no earlier than 20 May 2010 by its own content and no earlier than 2011 by its camera. It does not evidence the asserted 2007 origin of Metaconceptual Art, and it is not the earliest evidence in this collection: the 2008-2009 WTFIA folder is earlier in date, and the 2011-2012 postings remain the earliest evidence whose dates are assigned by a party other than the studio. The board's relation to the website www.wtfisart.com is one of shared wording, not of documented production: nothing here shows the board was made for the site or at the same time as it. No precedence over any other artist, work, or event is claimed from this record.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r1b14 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "A second work of the studio carries the same line. A camera-phone photograph dated 2011-11-08, described in the inscription-photograph record, shows a large handwritten sheet inscribed along its right edge '\"W.T.F. is Art ?\"', under a board carrying the phrase about the art historians of the future that the studio's museum letter of 2013-09-12 repeats. That sheet is a different object from this board: a different support, a different hand of the same writer, and different punctuation. It is dated nearly a year before the file that photographs this board, and the studio states that neither of those two physical works survives. This board is therefore not the only occurrence of the line in the studio's work, and it remains the earlier of the two to have reached a public platform.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r1b2 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The board accreted rather than being made at one sitting, so the span is a bracket, not a date of completion. Its lower bound is the latest datable thing pinned to it, the Paris museum theft of 20 May 2010; its upper bound is the moment the only photograph of it was taken. Where within that span any given line was written is not recorded.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r1b3 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object;
    rdfs:label "Artwork page";
    la:digitally_carried_by _:b0_r1b4.
_:b0_r1b4 <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/format> "text/html";
    a <http://www.ics.forth.gr/isl/CRMdig/D1_Digital_Object>;
    rdfs:label "Metaconceptual Art — Art Notes";
    la:access_point <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/artworks/art-notes>.
_:b0_r1b5 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Art Notes";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r1b6 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "work:art-notes";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r1b7 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "A framed studio notice board, half dry-erase panel and half cork, carrying handwritten notes in blue, black, red and green alongside pinned press cuttings and images. The written panel is headed 'Notes' over a drawn musical staff, and its first line reads 'WTF is Art ?!'. The same panel names 'Sun and Rain Works'. The board is the working surface on which the studio's questions were kept in view, and it is published here as a work in its own right: a note board about art that asks what art is, kept in the room where the work was made.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r1b8 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Transcription of the handwritten panel, line by line as laid out: 'Notes' (heading, over a drawn treble staff, with three drawn notes); '\"WTF is Art ?!\"'; '\"There's a time and a place for now\"'; '\"We have Art today!\"'; '\"For the love of God, We are th[...]!\"'; '\"There is this little thing called the Universe, Art is a part of it\"'; '\"Sun and Rain Works\"'; '\"Alien Highways\"'; '\"Time is space, Space is Time, Love is Eternal\"'. A drawn heart sits at the lower left. One line is partly effaced by camera flare in the only photograph held, and its final two words cannot be read with confidence; the ellipsis marks that gap rather than an omission in the original. Every line is given in the quotation marks the writer used.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r1b9 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Pinned to the cork half, as far as the photograph shows: a wire-service cutting headed 'Picasso fetches record price', reporting the sale of the 1932 painting 'Nude, Green Leaves and Bust' for $106.5 million at Christie's, annotated in ink in the margin with a date in the form '5-10'; a New York Times report by Doreen Carvajal headed 'Picasso, Matisse nabbed in theft at Paris museum', on the theft of five paintings from the Paris Museum of Modern Art, valued in the report at $114 million to $127 million; a cut-out reading 'Full Circle' beside a fragment reading 'PRINT THIS'; an illustrated street map of Lower Manhattan hand-lettered 'LOWER HEDGISTAN' with Broadway and Wall Street named, credited in the caption to Leif Parsons; a photograph of the Earth from space; a bald eagle's eye; a satellite image of cloud and coastline; and a detail of a hundred-dollar bill overlaid with two photographic portraits. The two press cuttings are third-party copyright material, incidental to the work and reproduced here only at a size at which their body text is not legible.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r20b0 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence archive: Sun and Rain Works in a market's membership records (2005, 2006, 2008)";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r20b1 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "record:evidence-saturday-market-membership";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r20b2 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence status: an institution speaking from its own records, and the earliest such statement in this collection. Its weight comes from three things. The market is not the studio and has no stake in the studio's claims. The years it gives are drawn from membership records it keeps for its own purposes, not from anything the studio supplied to it. And the statement is re-checkable: the market still exists, still holds the records, and can be asked again by anyone. Against that, one limit must be stated. The reply is held only as a screenshot of a mail client; the message itself has not been examined, no headers or checksum are published, and the exchange is therefore reported rather than verified in the way the 2013 museum correspondence has been. Obtaining the message file, or a written statement on the market's letterhead, would close that gap.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r20b3 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What it establishes, and what it does not. It establishes that a business trading as Sun and Rain Works existed and was selling work in Eugene, Oregon in 2005, six years before the earliest public use of the term Metaconceptual Art and three years before the studio's first documented approach to an art institution about the Adams photograph. It removes a question that would otherwise sit under every earlier claim in this collection, which is whether the studio existed as a working art business at the dates it describes. It does not touch Metaconceptual Art at all. A market's membership roll records who paid to sell at its stalls; it says nothing about what anyone thought, wrote, or made, and no part of it evidences the 2007 origin this collection asserts elsewhere. The market's records also say nothing about who held the membership beyond the business name.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r20b4 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "A coincidence, recorded and not interpreted. The years given are 2005, 2006 and 2008. The one year missing from that run is 2007, which is the year this collection asserts as the origin of Metaconceptual Art. Nothing is drawn from this. Market memberships lapse for ordinary reasons - a season away, a change of circumstances, a year not trading - and no document connects the gap to the assertion. It is noted here because a reader comparing the two dates will notice it, and because a record that omitted it would be less honest than one that does not.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r20b5 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Redaction. Following this collection's convention, the market's general manager, who answered the inquiry in an official capacity, is identified by role only; their name, direct mailbox and telephone number are withheld pending consent and retained in the studio's private copy. The organisation itself is named, because the identity of the attesting body is what gives the statement its weight. No endorsement by the Eugene Saturday Market of this project, of Metaconceptual Art, or of any claim in this collection is implied or should be inferred: the market answered a factual question about its own membership records and nothing more.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r20b6 crm:P14_carried_out_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    crm:P4_has_time-span _:b0_r20b7;
    a crm:E65_Creation;
    rdfs:label "Compilation of the market membership record".
_:b0_r20b7 crm:P82a_begin_of_the_begin "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    crm:P82b_end_of_the_end "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    a crm:E52_Time-Span;
    rdfs:label "2026".
_:b0_r21b0 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence archive: the approach to a museum photography department (11 August 2008)";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r21b1 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "record:evidence-sfmoma-approach-2008";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r21b10 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The closing 'For the Love of Art'. The studio's account, given in 2026, is that the sign-off was a deliberate adaptation of the title of Damien Hirst's For the Love of God (2007), with 'Art' in place of 'God', and not a chance valediction. This letter does not name Hirst, and 'for the love of' is an ordinary idiom, so the letter alone cannot confirm the reference; the studio did, however, use the unaltered title in its own later material, the notes panel and the wtfisart.com captures of 2014 and 2016. The evidence for and against the account is weighed in the record of the approach to Sotheby's, which closes with the same words three days later. The account of intent is reported as the studio's, not as a fact this letter establishes.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r21b11 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Two errors in the letter, recorded because they are evidence of the kind of document it is. The writer dates the photograph to 'what appears to be 1946'. The Sotheby's catalogue published seven months later gives 1952, printed probably in the 1950s. An earlier version of this record cited a shortcut file in the studio's archive, whose name carries the year 1946, as contemporaneous corroboration of that belief. That was wrong and is withdrawn: the shortcut was created on 2026-08-20 and points to a file on the present machine, so it records a recent naming choice and evidences nothing about 2008. The 1946 belief is attested by this letter alone, which is sufficient for the purpose - the letter is contemporaneous and states the belief in the writer's own words - and was later corrected by the auction house's cataloguing. Where the year came from is not recorded anywhere, and an explanation is now available which this record offers as a reading rather than as a fact. The verso of the print carries, in the photographer's printed credit block, the line 'NEGATIVE No. 1-SF-46'. That number is independently attested: the public catalogue entry for lot 16 of sale N08533 records the print as numbered 1-SF-46, and a photograph in which the whole line is legible is published with the documentation-photographs record. A writer examining the verso for information about a print he had just acquired, and finding a number ending in forty-six, might reasonably take it for a year; the letter's own hedge, 'from what appears to be 1946', is the phrasing of someone reading something off an object rather than recalling a fact he was told. Against that, nothing in the letter mentions the negative number, the inference is this collection's and not the writer's, and a person who misread a catalogue number as a date might equally have got the year from somewhere else entirely. The error is recorded here as explicable, not as explained. Second, the letter reports contact with a named person at 'the Ansel Adams Musem in SanFrancisco'. No institution of that name was operating in San Francisco in 2008: the Ansel Adams Center for Photography, run by the Friends of Photography, closed permanently in 2001 and the organisation dissolved. This is no longer an open question. The body the writer meant has since been identified: two messages of 2008-08-08, two days before this letter, were sent to a named representative at anseladams.com, which is the organisation bearing the photographer's name and is not a San Francisco museum. The contact this letter reports is therefore real and documented, and the error is confined to how the writer described the organisation. Neither error is material to anything this collection claims, and both are the ordinary imprecision of a person writing in real time rather than the tidiness of a document assembled afterwards.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r21b12 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Redaction and scope. The individual named in the letter as a contact at an Adams institution is not named here; that name is withheld pending consent, following this collection's convention, and is retained in the studio's private copy, as are the writer's then-current mailbox and telephone number. The recipient institution is named, because the identity of the body approached is what gives the approach its meaning. No interest, response, endorsement, or acquisition by the San Francisco Museum of Modern Art is claimed or should be inferred: this record evidences an unanswered letter. It establishes nothing about the 2007 origin asserted elsewhere in this collection, and nothing about the auction, which had not been proposed when it was written.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r21b13 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The artist's account of the word, recorded as his account and given in 2026. Sun & Rain Works states that the work was made under the influence of the Happenings of the 1960s, and that the word 'happening' in this letter was used in that sense deliberately. The statement differs from the studio's account of the concealment, recorded in the correspondence record, in one respect that matters: that account explained an absence, why nothing was said to the auction house, and could not be checked against anything. This one makes a claim about a specific word in a specific surviving document, and can therefore be set beside it. Two features of the letter are consistent with the account. The recipient is the photography department of a museum of modern art, which is the institutional home of that tradition rather than an incidental choice of correspondent. And the phrasing is marked: a writer meaning only an occurrence would more naturally have offered the museum the discovery or the photograph, whereas this sentence offers 'this happening' as the thing that might interest them, which is the situation rather than the object.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r21b14 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Why that account cannot settle the question it addresses, stated plainly because it is the central difficulty in this collection. What is at issue is whether a framing was present in 2008 or arrived later. An artist's testimony given in 2026 about what he meant in 2008 is not independent of that question: it is the thing being asked about. Memory reconstructs, and it does so sincerely. A person who came to understand an ambiguous choice as deliberate some years after making it will report, honestly and without any intention to mislead, that it was deliberate at the time. Nothing in this record suggests that is what happened here; the point is that this record cannot distinguish it from the alternative, and neither can the testimony, because the two produce identical statements. The account is therefore recorded, weighed as consistent with the document, and treated as carrying little evidential weight of its own. Only something written before the framing was in question could carry more.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r21b15 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "A relevant negative from the working archive. The studio's source folder for www.wtfisart.com, ninety-eight files assembled between August 2008 and March 2009 and searched exhaustively in 2026, contains no occurrence of the word happening, none of performance, and none of conceptual art. Its content is art-market press, dealer directories and art-historical reference plates. If the Happenings of the 1960s were the operative influence in that period, the folder carries no trace of that particular lineage. One qualification belongs here, and it cuts the other way. The folder is not empty of the studio's stated concerns: 'IsItArt.doc' compiles, among its twenty-four press reports, an article quoting Andy Warhol's dictum that good business is the best art - the central idea of the volume the studio had composed into its documentation photographs five months earlier. So a conceptual lineage is present in the contemporaneous papers; it is Warhol and art-as-commerce rather than Kaprow and the Happening. This should not be pressed too far in either direction: the folder was assembled for a website about the art market, and a person does not research what he already knows. But an influence claimed for this period leaves no mark in the only substantial body of the studio's own contemporaneous papers that survives, and a record that omitted that would be selecting its evidence.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r21b16 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence status, restated after the messages were produced as files. This letter and its sibling of 2008-08-14 are now held as message files exported from the studio's own mailbox, where previously both were held only as screenshots. Both retain original Hotmail headers: a provider-stamped X-Originating-IP, a Date carrying a real local offset of minus seven hours, and no Message-ID or Exchange organisation headers, so neither has passed through a later platform. Every phrase this record quotes has been checked against them and agrees word for word, including the description of the writer as an artist and as the sole proprietor of a fledgeling Art business, and the sentence about a happening. The letter also names, in full, the person at the Adams organisation whom this record previously described only as a named individual; that name is withheld here under this collection's convention, and the messages sent to them two days earlier are the subject of a separate record. Two limits stand: neither file carries a Received chain, which is ordinary for Sent Items copies, and no reply from the museum is held or evidenced.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r21b17 crm:P14_carried_out_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    crm:P4_has_time-span _:b0_r21b18;
    a crm:E65_Creation;
    rdfs:label "Compilation of the museum approach record".
_:b0_r21b18 crm:P82a_begin_of_the_begin "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    crm:P82b_end_of_the_end "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    a crm:E52_Time-Span;
    rdfs:label "2026".
_:b0_r21b2 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence status: held as a screenshot of a mail client, and therefore reported rather than verified. No message file has been examined, no headers or Message-ID inspected, and no checksum is published. The screenshot shows a quoted header block giving sender, recipient, a send time of Monday, August 11, 2008 at 4:01 PM, and the subject. The recipient is an institutional mailbox at a museum which may hold its own copy. The studio put a records request to the museum on 2026-09-01 asking whether its holdings reach this message; no reply had been received when this record was written. If the museum produces a copy, this record moves from reported to institutionally retained, as the 2013 correspondence did, and should be revised accordingly. If the museum searches and finds nothing, that negative finding should be recorded here with its scope stated, as the 2009 negative finding is recorded in the museum correspondence record. No reply from the museum is held, and nothing in this collection shows that the museum answered, acknowledged, or acted on the message. What is evidenced is that the approach was made, not that it was received by any particular person or had any effect.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r21b3 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "A second view of the same message, added 2026-09-10. A further screenshot shows the message itself, open in the sent mail of the same mailbox rather than quoted inside a forward: the same sender, recipient, subject, body and closing, the attachment 'San Francisco.zip' at 3 MB, and a banner recording that the studio forwarded the message on 2024-09-03. Two things follow. First, the original is still held in a live mailbox, so it can be exported as a message file with its full headers and Message-ID, which is the step that would move this record from reported to verified. Second, this view displays the send time as 3:01 PM, an hour earlier than the 4:01 PM of the quoted header block. The raw Date line of the sibling message described below, 'Mon, 11 Aug 2008 16:01:02 -0700', is 23:01 UTC, which a client displays as 4:01 PM at UTC minus seven and as 3:01 PM at UTC minus eight. The difference is therefore the client's display setting, not a second send time: this view renders times at UTC minus eight, although the date falls in Pacific daylight time. A display setting describes the viewer, not the sender, so it says nothing about where the message was written. The calibration is useful beyond this record, because the consignment agreement record has a timestamp that turns on the same client's offset. The 2024 forward is not otherwise documented in this collection.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r21b4 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "It moves two dates. Until this message was produced, the earliest dated instance of the name Sun and Rain Works in this collection was the approach to Sotheby's of 2008-08-14, and the earliest dated event in the auction engagement was the documentation session of 2008-08-08. This letter is dated 2008-08-11, so the name and the self-description as an artist and sole proprietor are now attested three days earlier than the approach to the trade. A further correction has since been required. This record previously said that the first documented outreach about the photograph was to a museum rather than to the trade, and that the museum was written to first. That is withdrawn. Two messages of 2008-08-08 to the Ansel Adams organisation precede this letter by two days, so the first documented outreach was to neither a museum nor the trade, and the order is: the Adams organisation, then the museum, then the auction house. What survives of the original point is narrower and still worth stating: the museum was approached before the auction house. The name Sun and Rain Works appears in neither earlier message, so this letter remains the earliest document carrying it. It is not a large interval, and both fall in the same week as the photographs.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r21b5 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "A sibling message of the same hour, and the first transmission header in this part of the collection. A further screenshot shows a message the studio forwarded on 2008-08-14 at 12:27 to a photographic-services address, carrying the same attachment, 'San Francisco.zip' at 3 MB. What it quotes beneath is not a rendered client view but a raw header block: sender, the subject 'Ansel Adams Finds Art in SanFrancisco', and 'Date: Mon, 11 Aug 2008 16:01:02 -0700'. Three things in that line check out independently. 11 August 2008 was in fact a Monday. The offset of seven hours behind UTC is Pacific Daylight Time, which is what Oregon was observing in August, agreeing with the Eugene address the letters give. And the clock time of 16:01 is the same minute the museum letter's client view reports. The header is displayed rather than verified from a message file, so this is a stronger class of screenshot rather than a different class of evidence; but a raw Date line with a correct weekday and a correct seasonal offset is materially better than a client's rendering of a date.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r21b6 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "That message is not the museum letter, and the difference should not be glossed. The museum letter opens 'Dear Sir or Madame'; this one opens with the first name of a named individual, and its body is shorter, omitting the paragraphs about ownership, research, the Portland appraisal contacts and the Adams institution. So the studio was sending the same subject line and the same attachment to more than one party within the same minute or two of the same afternoon, varying the salutation and the length. The header therefore corroborates the date, the hour and the timezone of the museum letter without being that letter's own header. Obtaining the museum letter as a message file was, when this passage was written, the thing that would verify it directly. That has since happened: the letter is now held as an exported message with its original Hotmail headers, and so is the sibling message, which quotes the letter's From, Subject and Date lines beneath its own and therefore attests the send time from a second file.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r21b7 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The word 'happening', and how much weight it can bear. The message says: 'I thought that this happening might be of some interest to SF MOMA.' Two readings are available and this record does not choose between them. On the first, 'happening' is the ordinary English noun for an event or occurrence, and the sentence means no more than that the writer thought the situation might interest the museum. On the second, 'happening' is the term of art for a constructed, participatory art event, associated with Allan Kaprow and current in exactly the institutional context being written to, and the sentence offers the situation itself, rather than the object, as the thing of interest. The second reading is available because of who was being addressed: a modern art museum's photography department, by a writer who has just called himself an artist. It is not compelled, because the sentence works perfectly well under the first reading and nothing else in the message develops the idea. This is the closest thing to a contemporaneous conceptual framing of the situation that this collection holds, and it is a single ambiguous word in a letter otherwise concerned with attribution, appraisal and significance.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r21b8 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The competing reading of the week as a whole, which should be weighed against the above. Within a few days in August 2008 the writer photographed the print, approached a museum, approached an auction house, sent the same letter and attachment to at least one further named individual and onward to a photographic-services address, reported contact with appraisers on the Portland art scene, and reported contact with a named person at an Adams institution. That is five or more parties in one week. That is the pattern of someone who believes he has found something valuable and is establishing what it is and what it is worth. The stated purpose in this letter is research into historical and artistic significance, and the appraisal contacts are about value. On that reading the approach to the museum is a request for expertise, which is an ordinary thing to ask a museum for, and carries no artistic intention at all. The breadth of the outreach supports this reading at least as well as the conceptual one, and a record that cited the word 'happening' without citing the surrounding behaviour would be selecting its evidence.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r21b9 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Where the letter went. The photograph is of San Francisco, the subject line places the finding there ('Ansel Adams Finds Art in SanFrancisco'), and the letter went to the modern art museum of that same city. Sun & Rain Works treats this as part of a deliberate framing, and the letter is consistent with that account. It does not discriminate between the two readings above, however: a person researching an Adams photograph of San Francisco would have had every reason to write to that city's museum of modern art first. What it does establish is narrower. The approach was addressed and composed rather than incidental - a chosen institution, a written headline, the same text sent to several parties within the week - which is evidence of deliberate outreach. It is not evidence of an intention, in 2008, to make the outreach an artwork, and should not be read as more.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r22b0 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence archive: the Sotheby's consignment correspondence (2008-2009)";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r22b1 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "record:evidence-sothebys-approach-2008";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r22b10 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The attachment as held. 'San Francisco.zip', 3,553,357 bytes, SHA-256 227328da2ffe06ef140d79515499ddf1451a5669abaf20c9f9e70d384ed36c43. It contains twelve entries: the eleven documentation frames DSCF0012 to DSCF0017 and DSCF0020 to DSCF0024, and a Windows thumbnail cache. Frames 0018 and 0019 are absent, the same gap the documentation-photograph record notes in the studio's loose copies. Every one of the twelve is byte-for-byte identical to the corresponding file in the studio's private source archive, so the archive's copies and the transmitted copies are the same data. The archive carries Info-ZIP UNIX extra fields, header id 0x5855, holding access and modification times as Unix epoch integers; these decode to 2008-08-08 07:05:36Z through 07:10:24Z for the eleven frames. Those are unambiguous UTC values, unlike the local-time fields a zip normally carries, and they sit seven hours after the camera's own Exif clock, an offset equal to Pacific Daylight Time, the zone of the Eugene, Oregon address the message gives.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r22b11 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Four independent agreements between the message and the photographs, each checkable. The message describes the attached photographs as made with a 1.3 megapixel FujiFilm camera; the frames carry camera-written Exif naming a FUJIFILM FinePix1300 and measure 1280 by 960 pixels, which is 1.23 megapixels, the rating the model is sold under. The message shows an attachment of 3 MB; the eleven frames and the thumbnail cache occupy 3,553,357 bytes, which a mail client displays as 3 MB. The message is dated 2008-08-14; the frames are dated 2008-08-08, six days earlier. The attachment is named for San Francisco; the lot is titled 'San Francisco from Twin Peaks'. This is the first point in the collection at which camera-written metadata on a studio-held file is corroborated by a separate document rather than only by itself. The corroboration is not independent of the studio, since both artifacts come from it, but it is independent of the files: the caveat that Exif can in principle be altered would now require the message to have been fabricated to match it.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r22b12 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What the archive cannot do. A zip file carries the timestamps of the files put into it, not a record of when it was made, so this one cannot date its own creation and does not by itself establish that anything was sent in 2008. Its container fingerprint is worth recording because it is unexpected: create-system 3 with Info-ZIP UNIX extra fields means the archive was written by Info-ZIP tooling on a Unix-like system, not by the Windows compressed-folder command, although the thumbnail cache inside it shows the source folder was a Windows one. Whether that reflects the machine used in 2008 or a later repackaging is not determinable from the file. The honest position is that the attachment corroborates the content of the message and the dating of the photographs, and that the date of transmission rests on the client display alone until the message itself is examined.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r22b13 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The Warhol frame was transmitted. DSCF0020, exposed at 00:08:46 by the camera's clock, shows a copy of 'The Philosophy of Andy Warhol (From A to B and Back Again)' stood upright and facing the camera against the framed print. The documentation-photograph record already described this frame and read it as the earliest dated trace of the thinking behind the concept of the auction as artwork. The attachment establishes something further about it: the frame was not kept in the studio. It went to the auction house, inside the set of photographs attached to the first approach. The message says nothing about the book. So what is evidenced is that an art-historical reference was deliberately composed into documentation sent to Sotheby's, and left unremarked. That is a gesture made outward rather than a private note, which is more than the record could previously say. It remains short of a designation: the message frames the print as property being researched, not as material of a work, and the auction that the designation concerns did not yet exist in August 2008.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r22b14 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Redaction and scope. Following this collection's convention, the Sotheby's specialist addressed in the message is identified by department and role only; their name and mailbox are withheld pending consent, as are the studio principal's then-current personal mailbox and telephone number, all of which are retained in the studio's private copy. The sothebys.com domain is deliberately retained, because the fact of an approach to that institution is the point of the record. The recipient institution may hold its own copy of this message; no copy has yet been obtained from it. What this record establishes is an approach, an identity, and a set of photographs. It establishes no acceptance, valuation, or endorsement by Sotheby's, no consignor of record, and nothing about the 2007 origin asserted elsewhere in this collection.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r22b15 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The thread continues to an executed contract. On 2009-03-18 the department sent a scanned consignment agreement, stating that a signed agreement was required before the photograph could be sold, and on 2009-03-19 the studio returned it signed as a PDF attachment. That file is held. It is described, checksummed and deliberately left unpublished in the consignment agreement record, which also sets out the two reasons it cannot be published: a confidentiality undertaking in the agreement itself, and a page of government identity documentation inside it.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r22b16 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The business was disclosed at first contact, and what that does and does not carry. The opening message to the auction house, of 2008-08-14, introduces its writer, in its first two sentences and before it reaches the photograph, as an artist living in Eugene, Oregon and as 'the sole proprietor of a fledgeling Art business, Sun and Rain Works'. So the auction house was told at the outset, in the same message that raised the print, that it was dealing with a working art business and with the person who was that business. Three consequences follow, and they should be kept apart. First, the name Sun and Rain Works is attached to this engagement from its first minute, not applied to it afterwards. Second, because a sole proprietorship has no legal personality separate from its proprietor, the consignment agreement executed in the proprietor's own name seven months later is the agreement of that business, made in the only name in which such a business can contract; the earlier statement in this collection that the contract evidences an individual rather than a business drew a distinction this business form does not support, and has been corrected. Third, and against the grain of the first two, the message does not say that the business is consigning anything. No consignment existed on 2008-08-14: the auction house made its offer the following day and the studio accepted a month after that. What the message states about the property is personal ownership and research. The business is the writer's declared identity in the exchange, not a stated party to a transaction that had not yet been proposed. One word in that self-description is recorded here rather than left for someone else to find. The business is called 'a fledgeling Art business' in August 2008, while the market's membership record places the same business at stalls in Eugene in 2005, 2006 and 2008, and this collection dates its founding to 2005. A concern three years old may reasonably call itself fledgeling, and small trading businesses commonly do, so the two statements are not in contradiction. They are in tension only to the extent that a reader expecting a settled enterprise will not find one described here, and the word is the studio's own, written at the time, to a party it was trying to impress. It is set down for the same reason the market record sets down the missing year of 2007: a reader comparing the documents will see it, and a record that passed over it would be less honest than one that does not.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r22b17 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The estimate agrees with the published catalogue, and anyone can check it. In the offer of 2008-08-15 the cataloguer proposed a provisional estimate of $7,000 to $10,000. The public catalogue entry for lot 16 of sale N08533, published some seven months later, carries an estimate of $7,000 to $10,000. That is a figure quoted in private correspondence before the catalogue existed, matching the figure the auction house later printed. It is the second point at which this studio-held correspondence can be tested against the public record without access to the archive, the first being the agreement of the verso markings in the 2008 photographs with the catalogue's physical description. The catalogue also prints the negative number 1-SF-46 and the American Trust Company commission, both of which appear in the studio's documentation.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r22b18 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The outcome of the sale is not recorded anywhere in this collection, and should not be assumed. The public catalogue page for lot 16 shows that bidding is closed but places the result behind an account login, so whether the lot sold, at what price, or whether it was bought in or withdrawn, is not established by any source held or consulted here. Nothing in this collection asserts a result, and no reader should infer one. The studio has been advised to ask the auction house for written confirmation of the outcome as part of a records request; until that is answered, the sale result remains an open question of fact.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r22b19 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "An earlier letter of the same week has since been produced, and it displaces part of what this record says about firsts. On 2008-08-11, three days before the approach to Sotheby's and under an identical subject line, the studio wrote to the photography department of the San Francisco Museum of Modern Art, repeating the same opening paragraph almost word for word. The earliest dated use of the name Sun and Rain Works in this collection is therefore that letter, not this one. The further claim once made here, that the first documented outreach about the photograph was to a museum rather than to the trade, is withdrawn: two messages of 2008-08-08 to the Ansel Adams organisation precede the museum letter by two days, so the first documented outreach was to the organisation bearing the photographer's name, and the museum was second and this letter third. This record remains the first approach to the auction house.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r22b2 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence status, restated after the messages were produced as files. Until 2026-09-20 every message in this thread was held only as a screenshot of a mail client, and this record said so and reported the thread rather than verifying it. Five of the studio's outbound messages are now held as message files exported from its own mailbox: the approach of 2008-08-14, the acceptance of 2008-09-16, the message of 2008-11-20, and the two of March 2009 that carry the consignment sheet and the executed agreement. Every phrase this record quotes has been checked against them and agrees word for word. Three limits stand. The files are the studio's own outbound copies, so the department's replies are held only as text quoted beneath them and no separately held copy of any reply exists here. They carry no Received chain, which is ordinary for Sent Items copies but means they are not transit records. And the auction house has not been asked to confirm anything. What they do carry is set out in the next passage, and it is stronger than anything this collection held before.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r22b20 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The artist's account of the concealment, recorded as his account and given in 2026. Sun & Rain Works states that the work was conceived under the influence of the Merry Pranksters, and that disclosing a conceptual framing to the auction house would have destroyed the form: the counterparty's unawareness is, on this account, part of the work rather than a gap in it. The proposition is coherent and it is locally grounded, since Ken Kesey farmed at Pleasant Hill, some fifteen miles from the Eugene market where the studio was trading in the same years. It also disposes of an argument that appeared in earlier versions of these records: the absence, across the trade correspondence, of any statement that the consignment was a work carries little weight, because that correspondence is with a cataloguer and an administrator about estimates, deadlines and signatures, and is not where such a framing would be put in any case. Two limits are recorded alongside it. The account is testimony given seventeen years after the events and is not contemporaneous evidence of what was intended in 2008 or 2009. And the model it invokes cuts both ways: the Pranksters concealed the prank from its subject while documenting it obsessively for themselves, so an appeal to that lineage explains the silence toward Sotheby's without explaining the silence in the studio's own contemporaneous papers, where the WTFIA folder of ninety-seven files, worked on until the week before the sale, never mentions the consignment. That internal absence remains the open question, and no account of the external one reaches it.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r22b21 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Two further messages extend the thread through the autumn of 2008. On 2008-09-17, on a raw header reading 'Wed, 17 Sep 2008 09:45:03 -0400', the cataloguer writes that there is no rush, that the deadline is January 2009, that cataloguing for the April sale begins in November, and that she will put the consignor in touch with the department's administrator, whom she names, because a code is required for the item to be admitted to the building and unsolicited packages are refused. She adds a personal line about having holidayed in the islands when her family lived in California. On 2008-11-20 the consignor replies, opening 'Aloha Lauren', that he will be in contact soon 'regarding the shipment of Ansel Adams from Twin Peaks in SanFrancisco to Sotheby's in NYC for the upcoming spring Art auction'. The phrase takes the print's title, 'San Francisco from Twin Peaks', and gives it to its maker: it is Ansel Adams, not the photograph, who is to be shipped from Twin Peaks, whether as shorthand or as wordplay. He is 'somewhat unsure' whether to keep the photograph in its original frame with its glass cover 'or have a professional art framer do the removal of the glass for increased safety and decreased likelihood of damage to the piece during shipment', says he can arrange either 'in the next week or two', and closes the paragraph: 'I look forward to talking with you further about this art in progress.' He writes that he is 'still on the islands, on Maui', and gives a post-office box there as his mailing address. The mail client displays the message as sent on Thu 11/20/2008 at 4:49 PM; the same client renders the museum letter of 2008-08-11 at UTC minus eight, so this is 00:49 UTC on 21 November. An earlier version of this record paraphrased the message as concerning the shipment of the photograph 'from San Francisco to New York'; the message's own words are quoted here instead. The header's weekday and its offset of four hours behind UTC, which is Eastern Daylight Time in September, are both correct.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r22b22 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Two identifications the thread now settles. The administrator whose first name appears in the offer letter of 2008-08-15, and who chases the signed agreement in March 2009, is named in full by the cataloguer on 2008-09-17, so the same member of staff runs through the correspondence from the first offer to the executed contract. And the Maui post-office box given on 2008-11-20 is the Hawai'i mailing address that the auction-engagement record describes as linking the consignor to the studio's domain and hosting receipts. That link had rested on the studio's own account of its papers; it is now visible in a message to the auction house. The box number itself is withheld here, as personal address data, and retained in the studio's private copy.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r22b23 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The register of the correspondence, weighed as a whole. The claim that this thread is purely commercial cannot be sustained in its strongest form. Across the eleven messages now known, and to three different recipients, the consignor's own language is persistently art-inflected: the museum letter of 2008-08-11 offers 'this happening' as something that might interest the institution; the approach to the auction house closes 'For the Love of Art'; the subject line under which the exchange with the auction house ran is 'Ansel Adams Finds Art in SanFrancisco', the same one used for the museum, and the auction house's replies carry it forward, though by the ordinary mechanics of replying rather than by any choice of theirs; the acceptance of 2008-09-16 speaks of consigning 'the Art', of 'the shipment of the art' and of 'this project'; the message of 2008-11-20 speaks of 'the upcoming spring Art auction', of 'a professional art framer' and of 'this art in progress'; the covering note returning the signed agreement in March 2009 allows an exception to saving paper 'for the purposes of Art' and signs off 'Yours in Art'. That is a run of such phrases over seven months, in a consistent voice, and it is not the voice of a person disposing of an asset. A limit on part of it: in 'consigning the Art' and 'the shipment of the art' the word names the print itself, and an Ansel Adams photograph is a work of art in the ordinary sense, so those two phrases do not distinguish sending an artwork to auction from making one; the auction house, answering in the same exchange, calls the object 'your Ansel Adams photograph'. To them should be added an act rather than a phrase: the signed consignment agreement was returned in a file named for the consignor with the word Art standing where his middle name belongs, which the consignment agreement record treats separately. What it is not, equally, is a designation. Every one of these is a turn of phrase inside a message about appraisal, shipping, glass removal or file formats. They are also not of equal weight, and a reader citing this record should know which sentence is carrying it. The opening declarations of 2008-08-14, that the writer is an artist and the sole proprietor of an art business, establish who was writing and nothing beyond it: an artist who owns a photograph and sends it to auction is doing what artists who own photographs ordinarily do, and those two sentences would read the same if no work were intended. 'Consigning the Art' and 'the shipment of the art' fare no better, for the reason given above. 'The upcoming spring Art auction', 'a professional art framer' and 'Yours in Art' are register rather than statement. What remains, and what this record now treats as the load-bearing phrase of the whole thread, is 'this art in progress', written on 2008-11-20, some four months before the sale. It adapts the fixed phrase 'work in progress', which means a work being made, by putting 'art' where 'work' belongs, the same substitution the studio made in the filename of the signed agreement and in the sign-off adapted from Hirst; read that way, it most naturally describes something being made rather than a matter being administered. An earlier version of this passage called the phrase at least as readable in the ordinary sense, an art matter currently under way; that sense remains available, but it is the weaker reading. Three limits hold it short of a designation, and they should travel with any citation of it. First, 'in progress' is ambiguous in ordinary English between a thing being made and a matter under way: a painting in progress is being made, a meeting in progress is not, and which sense applies turns on whether the head noun names something built or something that unfolds. 'Art' is both, which is why the phrase is suggestive and also why it does not settle the question. Second, the demonstrative has no secure antecedent, though the message now held as a file narrows the field considerably. The sentence runs: 'I will be able to arrange this either way in the next week or two, so I look forward to talking with you further about this art in progress.' It opens a paragraph of its own; the frame, the glass cover and the framer are discussed in the paragraph before it. So the candidates are not, as this record previously said, the photograph and its frame and glass and shipment sitting alongside the phrase in one paragraph. The sentence carries two demonstratives, and the first of them, 'arrange this either way', takes the framing decision. The second cannot take the same thing without redundancy, and the 'so' between them subordinates the framing to whatever the art in progress is. What remains open is whether that second 'this' reaches the print, the consignment, or something the writer did not name; if it reaches the print, the stronger reading still fails, because an Ansel Adams photograph of 1952 is finished art and not art in the making. Third, the phrase does not say what the work in progress is: the auction, the print's passage to it, or the website whose material the studio was assembling in the same months. A fourth limit stood here until 2026-09-20 and is now withdrawn. It said that the phrase, like every other message in this thread, was held only as a screenshot, so that the strongest contemporaneous phrase in this collection was also among its least hardened, and that exporting the message would change this. The message has since been exported. It carries a provider-stamped X-Originating-IP, a Date with a real local offset, and a References chain whose identifiers were written by the auction house's own mail server. The phrase is now among the best evidenced things here rather than the least. Two corrections of setting go with that. An earlier version of this passage placed the phrase inside the sentence about removing glass from a frame; a second placed it at the close of the paragraph discussing the glass. Both were wrong, both made the setting sound more administrative than it is, and both were written from a paraphrase rather than from the message. Two considerations hold the phrase up against those limits, and they are not of the same kind. The writer uses ordinary words for the print in the paragraph immediately before, 'the photograph' and 'the piece', so reaching for 'this art in progress' in the next breath is a marked choice against a vocabulary he had just been using. And the substitution is not isolated: the same person, in the same engagement, across seven months, put 'art' into 'work in progress', 'Art' into Hirst's 'For the Love of God', and 'Art' into his own name in the filename of the executed consignment agreement. Any one of the three is deniable as idiom or accident. The three together are a habit of composition showing in the documentary record, and that pattern, rather than the literal force of any single phrase, is the strongest thing this thread offers. The honest position is that the correspondence carries a sustained artistic self-presentation, one phrase within it that points past self-presentation toward making, and no statement that the auction is a work. All three parts of that should be reported by anyone citing this record.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r22b24 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The sign-off 'For the Love of Art', and the studio's account of it. The studio's account, given in 2026, is that the sign-off was chosen deliberately, as its own adaptation of the title of Damien Hirst's For the Love of God, the diamond-set platinum cast of a skull first exhibited in London in June 2007 with a widely reported asking price of £50 million, and not as a stock valediction. The objective features are consistent with that account. The phrase is that title with one word changed, 'Art' standing where 'God' belongs: the same substitution of the word Art into a fixed form that this record notes in 'this art in progress' and in the filename of the signed agreement. The work it adapts had, fourteen months earlier, made its price a subject of wide press coverage, and the letters carrying the sign-off concerned the appraisal and sale of a work of art. And the studio later used the unaltered title in its own material: the handwritten notes panel recorded in this collection carries 'For the love of God, We are th[...]!', and the captures of www.wtfisart.com of 2014 and 2016 read 'For the Love of God, We are the Art.' So the link between the phrase and Hirst's title is one the studio demonstrably made. Against that, and as plainly: neither message of August 2008 names Hirst or the skull; 'for the love of' is an ordinary English idiom; nobody receiving the messages was invited to see a reference; and the later uses show the link was made, not that it was made by 2008. The account of intent is the studio's own, given eighteen years afterwards, and is reported here as that account, not as a fact the messages establish. What the messages do establish is narrower: the sign-off closes both surviving approaches of August 2008, to two institutions three days apart, and 'Yours in Art' closes the note of March 2009, so it was a settled signature of the correspondence rather than a one-off.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r22b25 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The Warhol frame, and a loop that closes inside the studio's own contemporaneous papers. The volume composed with the print in DSCF0020, and transmitted with the other ten frames to a museum on 2008-08-11 and to the auction house on 2008-08-14, is 'The Philosophy of Andy Warhol (From A to B and Back Again)'. That book is the source of the best-known statement of art as commerce: its Business Art section runs 'I wanted to be an art business man or a business artist. Being good in business is the most fascinating kind of art', and continues 'making money is art and working is art and good business is the best art'. Two things follow that are matters of record rather than interpretation. First, the letters carrying those photographs introduce their writer, in their second sentence, as the sole proprietor of an art business - the position Warhol's passage names. Second, five months later the studio compiled into 'IsItArt.doc' a press article quoting that same dictum in full. So the book was owned and deliberately staged in August 2008, the self-description in the covering letters matches its central idea, and the idea reappears in the studio's own research file in January 2009. The engagement with that specific thesis is documented across the period rather than asserted about it.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r22b26 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What the Warhol frame is, and what it still is not. The studio's account is that the book was placed as an obvious self-reference. The objective features support a deliberate gesture rather than incidental clutter: the volume is propped upright and square to the camera on the same ground as the print, in one frame of eleven, and the set was sent onward to two art institutions. Read against the transaction it accompanies, pairing the object being consigned to an auction house with the canonical text on art as business is precise rather than decorative, and the record of this collection identified that frame as the earliest dated trace of the thinking behind the concept of the auction as artwork before the studio said anything about it. Against that: the gesture was unremarked in every message, so nobody at either institution was invited to read it, and a cataloguer receiving eleven photographs of a print would reasonably see a book. Whether an unremarked gesture is a communication or a private signature is exactly the question the studio's account of concealment raises, and this record does not settle it. What it can say is that the gesture is deliberate, dated, transmitted, and thematically exact.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r22b27 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The archive was not first sent here. San Francisco.zip, whose SHA-256 is published above as the 3 MB attachment to the message of 2008-08-14, is byte-identical to the attachment of a message sent to the Ansel Adams organisation on the evening of 2008-08-08. The same archive therefore went to the organisation bearing the photographer's name six days before it went to the trade, and existed in its final form within a day of the photographs being made. Nothing about the auction engagement turns on this, but any account of the archive that began with this message would be starting in the middle.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r22b28 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The filename is attested in the sender's own words, on a dated message. This collection treats JoeArtChirum.pdf, the name under which the executed consignment agreement was returned, as an act rather than a phrase: the word Art standing where the consignor's middle name belongs. Until the message files were produced, that gesture rested on a file held on the studio's own media. It now rests on the covering message of 2009-03-19 as well, which says 'I have attached a pdf file: JoeArtChirum.pdf' in its body, allows an exception to saving paper 'for the purposes of Art', and closes 'Yours in Art'. The attachment on that message is byte-identical to the held file, SHA-256 970c8e85bf0931ac8bfed1ab742d7f67247cdb2003c4db4aea99b9b29138eabe, which this collection had already published. So the naming is contemporaneous, deliberate enough to be typed out in prose, and dated by a message whose headers were written in 2009. What it still is not is a designation of the auction as a work, and the message says nothing of the kind.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r22b29 crm:P14_carried_out_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    crm:P4_has_time-span _:b0_r22b30;
    a crm:E65_Creation;
    rdfs:label "Compilation of the 2008 approach record".
_:b0_r22b3 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The auction house's own mail server corroborates the thread. The messages of 2008-11-20 and March 2009 carry In-Reply-To and References headers, and those headers hold eleven message identifiers generated by NYCEXC01.ads.sothebys.com, which is the auction house's own Exchange server, alongside identifiers generated by the sending provider at phx.gbl. Nothing in this collection produced those eleven strings. They were written by the receiving institution when it replied, and ordinary mail threading copied them verbatim into the studio's next message. Two unrelated systems therefore attest the same exchange, which is the same form of corroboration the 2013 museum correspondence rests on, where identifiers written by one provider reappear in a university server's headers. It is the strongest corroboration in this collection, and unlike everything else here it is checkable by a party other than the studio: anyone with access to the auction house's archive can look those identifiers up. Two things it is not. It is not a statement by the auction house about this project or about Metaconceptual Art, and no endorsement by it is implied or should be inferred. And it corroborates that the exchange happened, on the dates and in the order recorded; it says nothing about what the consignment was.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r22b30 crm:P82a_begin_of_the_begin "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    crm:P82b_end_of_the_end "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    a crm:E52_Time-Span;
    rdfs:label "2026".
_:b0_r22b4 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The thread, message by message. 2008-08-14, studio to Sotheby's: the approach described above, with the zip attached. 2008-08-15, Sotheby's to studio, opening 'As per our recent conversation': an offer to include the Adams 'San Francisco from Twin Peaks' in the April 2009 sale at a provisional estimate of $7,000 to $10,000, 'bearing in mind that it is in perfect condition'; a deadline of the end of January 2009; an instruction that the print be arranged through the department before sending, since unsolicited packages are turned away; the first name of the administrator who would supply the forms; and an attachment of consignment charges. The phrase 'as per our recent conversation' records a telephone call between the two messages of which nothing else survives. 2008-09-15, Sotheby's to studio, on a header timestamped 13:56:41 -0400 and under the subject 'RE: Ansel Adams Finds Art in SanFrancisco': a follow-up about 'your Ansel Adams photograph', asking whether the owner still wishes to consign it to the April 2009 auction. 2008-09-16, studio to Sotheby's, under the same subject: 'I am interested in consigning the Art in the April 2009 auction'; a request for the deadlines; a note that the writer is visiting Hawai'i at that time; an undertaking to 'arrange for the shipment of the art myself'; and thanks for the 'reminder of this project'. The client view of that reply shows it as sent Tue 9/16/2008 at 1:55 PM, records that it was forwarded on 2024-06-05, and quotes the 2008-09-15 message beneath it with its raw header lines.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r22b5 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What the auction house's own words settle. In the 2008-08-15 and 2008-09-15 messages Sotheby's names the object by the exact title under which it was later catalogued as lot 16 of sale N08533, values it, and proposes to sell it for the person it is writing to. That is the auction house, not the studio, attesting the consignment relationship. Taken with the documentation photographs, the verso agreement with the public catalogue entry, and the transmitted attachment, the consignment ceases to be a matter of the studio's account. Two further details follow from the thread. The writer's presence in Hawai'i in September 2008 agrees with the Hawai'i mailing address that links the consignor to the studio's domain records. And the sale was planned throughout this correspondence as an April 2009 sale, whereas N08533 was held on 30 March 2009 - a schedule that moved, and a fact that bears on the misdated month in the 2013 museum letter, which the correspondence record treats separately.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r22b6 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What the thread does not contain. In none of its messages, though the studio had every occasion to say so, does the thread state that the consignment or the sale is an artwork, a performance, or a conceptual project. The word 'project' does occur once, in the acceptance of 2008-09-16, which thanks the auction house for its 'reminder of this project'; the reminder it answers was a query about consigning the print, so the word names the consignment matter without saying what kind of project it is. The auction house's side is ordinary trade throughout: estimates, deadlines, forms and shipping. The studio's side deals with the same practical matters in a vocabulary of its own, set out in the note on the register of the correspondence below: 'the Art', 'the upcoming spring Art auction', 'a professional art framer', 'this art in progress', 'Yours in Art'. Earlier versions of this record said that nothing in the thread frames the consignment as an artwork, that its only art-inflected touches were 'the Art' and a sign-off, and that it is not evidence that the engagement was framed as art at the time. All three statements are withdrawn as too strong. The thread is strong evidence of the consignment, of the studio's identity as a working art business, and of the studio presenting the engagement as art while it was under way. It is not a statement that the auction itself was a work, which is the designation this collection records: the messages of March 2009 fall within three weeks of the sale, but they are administrative, a chase for a signed agreement, a reply about post, and the return of the signed contract. The earliest explicit statement in this collection that the sale was a work is the museum letter of 2013.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r22b7 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The consignment agreement, March 2009. On 2009-03-11, nineteen days before the sale, the administrator of the Photographs department writes from a sothebys.com address, on a header timestamped 'Wed, 11 Mar 2009 12:01:52 -0400', under the subject 'in regards to consignment agreement for SOTHEBY'S'. She says the catalogue should have arrived and that she hopes the recipient is pleased with the presentation of 'your photograph'; that the sale is rapidly approaching and no signed consignment agreement has come back; that she posted it at the very beginning of February; and that she will issue a new one if needed. Her signature block carries the department's sale line, 'Photographs March 30, 2009'. The following day the studio replies that the consignment sheet was received but the catalogue was not, that the sheets will be faxed, and that post is reaching a general-delivery address in Eugene because forwarding to Maui has been slow. The administrator who writes here is the member of staff the 2008 offer letter said would supply the consignment forms, so the two ends of the thread name the same person.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r22b8 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The consignor of record. This collection has until now recorded that the identity of the consignor was not established, on the ground that the published catalogue names no consignor, as is standard auction practice. That caveat can now be narrowed. In these two messages the auction house's own Photographs department, writing to the studio principal's mailbox nineteen days before the sale, calls the object 'your photograph', refers to 'your catalogue' and to 'your consignment agreement', and chases the signed agreement it had posted in February; the studio answers that it holds the consignment sheet and will return it. A consignment agreement is the instrument by which a consignor consigns. Two things should still be said. The signed agreement itself is not held and is not published here, so what is evidenced is the auction house treating this person as its consignor, rather than the executed contract. And the catalogue remains silent on the point, so a reader working only from the public record still cannot see it. That is why the statements elsewhere in this collection that the catalogue does not name Sun & Rain Works are correct, and they are left standing.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r22b9 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Location, across the whole run of evidence. The 2008 opening message gives Eugene, Oregon. The market membership record places the same business at stalls in Eugene in 2005, 2006 and 2008. In September 2008 the studio writes from Hawai'i. In March 2009 it is receiving post at a general delivery address in Eugene while mail is being forwarded, slowly, to Maui. The Hawai'i mailing address that links the consignor to the studio's domain records is on Maui. These are four documents, two of them from third parties, describing one person moving between the same two places across four years, and they agree with each other. Personal addresses are withheld here.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r23b0 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence archive: the staged photographs of the print (autumn 2008)";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r23b1 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "record:evidence-staged-photographs-2008";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r23b10 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What the staging can and cannot carry. The studio's account is that these are theatrical photographs, made by its art assistants as documentation of WTF is Ansel Adams. The frames support more of that account than any earlier document in this collection. The storage frames are directed rather than incidental: a pose at a door with the keys held up to the lens, the print lifted in the doorway and then presented squarely to the camera, the same subject arranged in several settings. They stage a finding, and the subject line under which the studio had approached a museum and an auction house three months earlier was 'Ansel Adams Finds Art in SanFrancisco'. Five days before the Mac's date on these files, the studio described the matter to the auction house as 'this art in progress'. The ordinary reading is also available and should be stated with equal force. A person who has written that he owns a previously unknown piece, and who is preparing to consign it, has every reason to photograph where it was kept, its condition, its verso, and its removal from the frame for shipment; the verso frames and the workshop frames are documentation of exactly that kind, and the exterior frames record a place. On that reading the posed frames are a record of the retrieval, however it was staged. This record does not choose between the readings. It records that the series is staged, that it was made before the sale, and that it was kept inside the studio.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r23b11 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What this record does not show. None of these frames was among the photographs sent to the museum on 2008-08-11 or to the auction house on 2008-08-14, which were the eleven frames of 2008-08-08, and nothing in this collection shows that anyone outside the studio saw them in 2008 or 2009. They are not evidence of a public act, or of the auction house's knowledge of any artistic framing. They carry no statement, caption or title that designates the retrieval or the sale as a work. They say nothing about the 2007 origin asserted elsewhere in this collection. And their dates bound the files' processing, not their capture.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r23b12 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Checksums of the files as held, SHA-256, in frame order, with sizes in bytes. P1010001.jpg, 74069, 6f6f8a275f3abeefb6367c8957113da5f56af2f56b72a74ed43ef426f3a0d07f. P1010002.jpg, 175808, bc0230d510b6215f213948126387604e06145fd3bff711387d6b406e6c67efd5. P1010003.jpg, 94724, 6576acb01fbd1b50373ce81a45492dd9c65a4c98e8ee75c3e04c1e6d9bec29bb. P1010004.jpg, 206201, fcac5f5fdc1190c21023ddd665ca91ff1fcad81157669e66179bdc3ab3f7b5f8. P1010005.jpg, 149612, 7d4ea9963f16bdecb741f5d3ad75af31ec641811a3dc34577638198ab168bbec. P1010006.jpg, 184480, edf9c5866cebb0dd7284a70fe01f218d940ab769075f71cd257d07d3a72197ac. P1010007.jpg, 163712, be80e16ba65497bf40cce29e3e15c5593fe069cdd289c5d441aeec07f04294b9. P1010008.jpg, 61155, cd7ea0235ca6253063bd2d28c7bf7dec62bdc52c91ae50873406c175d0b86951. P1010009.jpg, 79555, f98de276c083b48584e5a8f13a9482196fe545cdf2436fed95155bc86a5c3eea. P1010010.jpg, 81912, d24b75acb9d06910c7f49aacba9982e815b37afdbbc3589f5d9627fc6d695831. P1010011.jpg, 188033, 125fd7e651fe82f00b6f01957de009cd85d3ea33594e31b98064004f6b9df128. P1010012.jpg, 77987, 208eb6aed3e93a6c4965ecfca1193e263c4a7934d7fcea1a5dcf53deefe5ba62. P1010013.jpg, 168902, 9c6f78a4f3a5cd167f11995796b8cf5ff02ee5cc7dbeecb7ebf7e57195a38247. P1010014.jpg, 122861, fb453d0731f0c5553520742ecadcabec54a9a8d54393ae86dfc4098397def78f. P1010015.jpg, 156075, ff0c4d2cbaaaa24576d24c775e0e3294628bdc9af19eeca92560fbbc8e8cfde7. P1010016.jpg, 180671, 3eb318a632d6d5901f368dfceb11015367d2c6c3df3911a273fcd7caa0e55883. P1010017.jpg, 300811, 25b1917d2b95fd8ad7fb1eb895be58e623dbc1455aab7e89740020d4e90fda3d.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r23b13 crm:P14_carried_out_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    crm:P4_has_time-span _:b0_r23b14;
    a crm:E65_Creation;
    rdfs:label "Compilation of the staged photographs record".
_:b0_r23b14 crm:P82a_begin_of_the_begin "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    crm:P82b_end_of_the_end "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    a crm:E52_Time-Span;
    rdfs:label "2026".
_:b0_r23b2 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence status: held by the studio; reported, with one clock-independent bound. The seventeen files are held in the studio's photography folder, which sits beside the WTFIA folder on the same medium. That folder also holds the eleven frames of 2008-08-08 described in the photographs record, and one downloaded photograph by another photographer, which is not part of this series and not a studio original. The files' metadata is their own account and is not notarized, with one exception described below: a software version, which dates the files' processing without relying on anyone's clock. The images are not published. They show the face of a person who is not the studio principal and has not consented to publication, the face of a second, unidentified person, a readable vehicle registration plate and the exterior of a private residence. The SHA-256 checksum of every file is published below, so that the held files can later be shown to be unaltered without being disclosed.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r23b3 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What the frames show, in the camera's frame order. P1010001: a person poses at a white door, holding a ring of keys up to the camera. P1010002: the same person, inside a storage unit walled with wire mesh and crowded with boxes and a bicycle, holds up a pale cloth. P1010003: taken from behind, the person stands in the unit's doorway, a padlock hanging on the frame, and lifts the framed print. P1010004 and P1010005: the print's image, and the framed print propped upright, both lit by an on-camera flash. P1010006 and P1010007: the framed print leaning against a railing on the plank floor of a warehouse mezzanine. P1010008 and P1010009: the person, in the same space, holds the print squarely to the camera. P1010010 and P1010012: the verso, carrying the 'Photograph by Ansel Adams for The American Trust Company' stamp, the ink annotation 'Wells Fargo Bank', 'Negative No. 1-SF-46' and the title 'San Francisco from Twin Peaks', the same verso recorded in the photographs of 2008-08-08. P1010011: the person, in a corridor lined with chain-link storage cages, holds the print and looks into the camera. P1010013 and P1010014: in a framing workshop, an older man takes the print out of its frame at a workbench and then examines it on its mount. P1010015 to P1010017: a shed and carport in a garden, with fallen leaves on the grass.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r23b4 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "How the series is dated, and how firmly. The camera, an Olympus C-3020Z, wrote no capture time: every DateTimeOriginal and DateTimeDigitized field reads 0000:00:00 00:00:00, which is what such a camera writes when its clock has never been set. The file names agree: this camera builds each name from the month and day, and 'P101' corresponds to 1 January, the date an unset clock reports. Every file was then rewritten by software, which left three fields: Software 'QuickTime 7.5.5', HostComputer 'Mac OS X 10.5.5', and a modification time running from 2008-11-25 18:56:10 to 18:56:17 in frame order, by that computer's clock, which records no timezone. Three bounds follow, of different strength. The floor is independent of any clock: QuickTime 7.5.5 was released on 2008-09-09 and Mac OS X 10.5.5 on 2008-09-15, so the files cannot have been processed by that pairing before 15 September 2008. The Mac's own date, 25 November 2008, falls within the months when 10.5.5 was the current release, before 10.5.6 arrived on 15 December 2008, and is consistent with it; it remains a clock reading, not a verified date. The ceiling is the file system's record of the copy into the studio's working folder, 2009-01-20 between 13:45 and 13:48, which is self-reported, and which falls two days before the WTFIA documents were begun by their own metadata. Two details in the frames agree with the autumn: fallen leaves lie on the grass in the exterior frames, and the workshop frames show a step the studio was planning in writing on 2008-11-20, as described below. None of this dates the capture itself, which could in principle precede the processing; it dates the files as they are held.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r23b5 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The workshop frames, and a message five days earlier by the Mac's clock. On 2008-11-20 the studio wrote to the auction house's cataloguer about the shipment 'to Sotheby's in NYC for the upcoming spring Art auction', 'somewhat unsure' whether to keep the photograph in its original frame with its glass cover 'or have a professional art framer do the removal of the glass', as the Sotheby's correspondence record quotes. Frames P1010013 and P1010014 show the print being taken out of its frame at a framer's workbench and examined on its mount, in a workshop the studio identifies as Raven Frame Works, a framing business in Eugene, Oregon; the man at the workbench is not named. The same message gave a mailing address on Maui, and the studio identifies the person in the storage frames as its art assistant rather than its principal, so in these frames the print is being handled by others on the studio's behalf. The studio states that it directed the retrieval and the unframing remotely from Hawai'i, working with its art assistant and the framer, and that doing so was the composition of WTF is Ansel Adams. The documents bear out where the studio was and who handled the print; that the handling was the making of a work is the studio's account, given in 2026. The message and the files are both held by the studio, so neither is independent of the other's source. They are, however, two different kinds of record, one a message describing a plan and the other photographs of that plan being carried out, and they agree.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r23b6 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The approach to the framer, and what it has produced so far. On 2024-09-05 the studio wrote to the proprietor of the framing business to ask whether he was the same person, and he replied the following day that he is. On 2024-09-06 the studio wrote again: it described the 2008 job, linked the auction house's public catalogue entry for lot 16, attached the workbench frame from this series, and asked whether the original receipt for the work survives. That second message was not answered. The studio wrote again on 2026-09-18, asking only whether the original receipts or any record of work done in 2008 survived, and was answered the same afternoon: 'I do not have any Raven Frame Works paperwork from 2008.' What the exchange establishes is therefore his identity, and a search of his own records that found nothing. It does not confirm the job, its date, or its purpose, and no record from the business has been produced. He has not been asked whether he wishes to be named, and is not named here. A surviving work order, or a few sentences of recollection in writing, would be the first account of any part of this series from outside the studio; until there is one, the series is dated by the studio's own files alone.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r23b7 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The framer's answer, and exactly how far it reaches. The reply of 2026-09-18 is a negative finding and must be read as one. He says he holds no Raven Frame Works paperwork from 2008. He does not say that no work was done, that the print never came to his bench, or that he has no memory of it; he was asked about documents and he answered about documents. A small business not holding paperwork from eighteen years earlier is unremarkable and evidences nothing either way about the job. So the position after this exchange is the position before it, with one thing removed: the possibility that a surviving work order would settle the series' date from outside the studio. That possibility is now closed, and the series continues to rest on the studio's own files and on the clock-independent floor described above. What the exchange adds is small but real. He confirmed in 2024, in his own words, that he is the Raven Frame Works proprietor, and he has now answered a second time, so the business and the person the studio names are not a supposition. A few sentences of recollection in writing would still be the first account of any part of this series from outside the studio. He has not been asked for one, he has not been asked whether he wishes to be named, and he is not named here.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r23b8 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "A note on the evidential quality of that reply, because it is unlike everything else here. The message is held as an inbound file as the studio's mail provider received it, not as a copy from a sender's outbox. It therefore carries a full Received chain and, unusually for this collection, cryptographic authentication: the provider's own headers record dkim=pass against the sending domain's published key, spf=pass for the sending host, and arc=pass. A DKIM signature covers the message body and principal headers, so a passing result means the message was not altered between the sender's provider and the recipient's. Every message in the 2008 and 2009 correspondence is a Sent Items copy with no Received chain and no signature, and is trusted on the strength of circumstantial agreement. This one is verifiable in a way none of those are. The point is worth stating plainly and also worth bounding: what the signature authenticates is that this person sent this text on this date. It authenticates nothing about 2008.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r23b9 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Who is pictured, and who held the camera. The person in the storage frames is identified by the studio as one of its art assistants; that name is withheld here pending their consent and is retained in the studio's private copy. The older man at the workbench is not identified in this record. The photographer is not pictured and is not named by the files, whose metadata carries no artist field. One frame, P1010003, is taken from behind the person pictured as they step into the storage unit, so at least that frame was made by someone other than its subject. Sun & Rain Works states that the series was photographed by its art assistants.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r24b0 crm:P2_has_type <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/>;
    crm:P75i_is_possessed_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    a crm:E30_Right;
    rdfs:label "CC BY 4.0".
_:b0_r24b1 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence archive: the WTFIA source folder for www.wtfisart.com (2008-2009)";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r24b2 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "record:evidence-wtfia-2009";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r24b3 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence status: studio-held, with self-describing file metadata. The dates given here are drawn from filesystem modification times and from the OLE document-summary metadata embedded in the Word files. They are internally consistent across the ninety-seven files in the folder, but they are reported by the files themselves and are not independently notarized; they should be read as the archive's own account of its dates. The press sources excerpted in 'IsItArt.doc' are independently verifiable at their published URLs. No checksums, file hashes, or custody identifiers are published at this time. The same storage medium also holds an unrelated 2009 website project on renewable energy, 'Growing Aloha'; it is outside the boundary of this evidence archive.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r24b4 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Embedded document metadata, as recorded by the files themselves: 'IsItArt.doc' gives author Joseph M. Chirum, created 2009-01-22T03:03, last saved 2009-01-28T21:23, revision 21, total editing time 09:46, 14 pages, 4,049 words, 23,082 characters; its Title field carries the string 'August 13, 2007', which is a metadata field value and not, by itself, evidence of a 2007 document. That string is now accounted for: 'August 13, 2007' is the date of one of the twenty-four press reports compiled inside the document itself, a New York Sun piece by Kate Taylor headed 'Seeking A Hedge For Art'. The Title field took the value of a heading in the body, as Word does, and carries no authorship date of its own. 'Photographers.doc' gives author Joseph M. Chirum, created 2009-01-22T04:19, last saved 2009-01-26T04:15, revision 4, 1 page, 69 words. 'OldMasters.doc' gives created 2009-01-23T18:22, last saved 2009-01-26T20:50, revision 19, 8 pages, 1,263 words. 'Modern.doc' gives created 2009-01-27T13:15, last saved 2009-01-28T03:49, revision 10, and was produced with a different word processor from the other three. A second contributor is recorded in the metadata of all four documents: as the named author of both dealer-network directories, 'OldMasters.doc' and 'Modern.doc', and as the last editor of all four files. That person's name is withheld from this public record pending their consent, and is retained in the studio's private copy; the attribution of the two directories is theirs, not the studio principal's.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r24b5 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The folder is named for the 'WTF is Art' concept and holds the material Sun & Rain Works assembled for www.wtfisart.com: the art-market press compilation, the photographer note, and the two dealer-network directories of galleries and their web addresses that were built as the site's link content. Its art-market compilation is the documented source context for the image study Art Market Reform, and the dealer directories are a documented instance of the project's stated interest in an archaeology of the art world. The folder holds no HTML: the built pages of www.wtfisart.com are not among these files, so the folder evidences what was made for the site, not what the site published or when it went live. The folder also contains an Ansel Adams reference image dated 2009-01-21 and a photographer reference note dated 2009-01-25; these show that Ansel Adams and photographic authorship were present in the same 2009 working set, and nothing further. The 2009 Sotheby's consignment is evidenced separately, in the auction-engagement evidence record. One temporal alignment is worth recording: the latest-dated material in the folder - the modern-gallery directory, last saved 2009-03-23, and the seventeen art-market images, all dated 2009-03-23 - falls one week before the Sotheby's Photographs sale of 30 March 2009 at which lot 16 was offered. The folder was therefore in active use in the week before that sale. This places the work and the sale in the same period; it does not show that the material was made for the sale, and no such intent is claimed from the dates alone. Each item here is scoped to what it shows.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r24b6 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Contents of the documents, as counted in 2026 when they were transcribed in full: 'IsItArt.doc' holds twenty-four dated press reports running from 13 May 2000 to 29 January 2009, twenty-two of them carrying the source link the compiler recorded and two carrying none; 'OldMasters.doc' holds sixty-four dealers and galleries, every one with a web address, grouped under seventeen regional headings; 'Modern.doc' holds eighty-one galleries under twenty-nine regional headings with no web addresses at all, its first group of five carrying no heading; 'Photographers.doc' holds the single note on Lee Balterman. These counts describe the documents as held and are independent of any claim about the website.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r24b7 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "An exhaustive negative search, run 2026-09-01 and recorded so that it need not be repeated. All ninety-eight files on the studio's source medium, totalling 20,271,324 bytes, were searched for any trace of the 2009 auction engagement. The method was not a reading of the readable files but an extraction of every ASCII and every UTF-16 string from every byte of every file, so that text embedded in the Word documents' internal streams, in Photoshop layers, in the thumbnail caches and in the images' own metadata segments would be caught. The following terms return no hit anywhere in the folder: consign, consignment, N08533, lot 16, Twin Peaks, appraisal, hammer, estimate, and the surname of either member of Sotheby's staff. So do the terms metaconceptual, meta conceptual, wtfisart, WTF is Art, and Sun and Rain, none of which appears in the folder at all, including in the folder named for the first of them. The word Sotheby appears only inside the press compilation, in the headline and body of art-market reports about the auction houses, and the word auction likewise, together with one gallery whose trading name contains it. The words Ansel and Adams appear only in the filename of a reference plate and in a shortcut created in 2026. The conclusion is the one this record already stated and can now state as tested rather than assumed: the working material assembled for www.wtfisart.com, worked on until the week before the sale of 30 March 2009, contains no mention of the consignment, of the sale, or of the concept later called Metaconceptual Art.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r24b8 crm:P14_carried_out_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    crm:P4_has_time-span _:b0_r24b9;
