_:b0_r31b0 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Sotheby's Photographs auction (sale N08533, 2009)";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r31b1 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "activity:sothebys-photographs-auction-2009";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r31b2 crm:P82a_begin_of_the_begin "2009-03-30T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    crm:P82b_end_of_the_end "2009-03-30T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    a crm:E52_Time-Span;
    rdfs:label "30 March 2009".
_:b0_r31b3 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The public Sotheby's Photographs auction (sale N08533), New York, 2009, carried out by Sotheby's, at which Sun & Rain Works consigned an Ansel Adams photograph, 'San Francisco from Twin Peaks' (lot 16). Sun & Rain Works designates its engagement with this auction as the material of the concept of the auction as artwork, realised in the works www.wtfisart.com and WTF is Ansel Adams. This record names the real-world event and cites the public catalogue; it does not assert that Sotheby's authored, endorsed, or participated in the artwork, and the catalogue does not name Sun & Rain Works.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r31b4 a crm:E53_Place;
    rdfs:label "Sotheby's, New York".
_:b0_r32b0 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Sotheby's";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r32b1 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "agent:sothebys";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r32b2 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "https://www.sothebys.com/";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404626;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r32b3 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "International auction house founded in 1744. Named here only as the institution that carried out the Sotheby's Photographs auction (sale N08533), New York, 2009 — the public event that Sun & Rain Works designates as the material of the concept of the auction as artwork, realised in the works www.wtfisart.com and WTF is Ansel Adams. Recording Sotheby's as the auction's host asserts no partnership, endorsement, or claim against Sotheby's.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r33b0 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Sun & Rain Works";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r33b1 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "agent:sun-and-rain-works";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r33b2 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The art business and studio established in 2005 that authors Metaconceptual Art and holds its copyright.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r33b3 crm:P4_has_time-span _:b0_r33b4;
    a crm:E66_Formation;
    rdfs:label "Formation of Sun & Rain Works".
_:b0_r33b4 crm:P82a_begin_of_the_begin "2005-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    crm:P82b_end_of_the_end "2005-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    a crm:E52_Time-Span;
    rdfs:label "2005".
_:b0_r34b0 crm:P14_carried_out_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    crm:P15_was_influenced_by mca:auction-as-artwork;
    crm:P4_has_time-span _:b0_r34b1;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by _:b0_r34b2;
    crm:P9i_forms_part_of mca:production-live-auction-2009;
    a crm:E12_Production;
    rdfs:label "Designation of WTF is Ansel Adams".
_:b0_r34b1 crm:P82a_begin_of_the_begin "2009-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    crm:P82b_end_of_the_end "2009-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    a crm:E52_Time-Span;
    rdfs:label "2009".
_:b0_r34b10 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "A work in its own right. Earlier versions of this record modelled WTF is Ansel Adams as a part of a titled work, 'The Auction as Artwork'. There was no such titled work; that phrase names the concept the studio's two works around the 2009 engagement share, and the correction is set out in the record for that concept. This work is recorded as a work, related to www.wtfisart.com, and its making is recorded as influenced by the concept rather than as a part of it. The origin claim for 2009 names www.wtfisart.com as its work and this one as the related work, because the website's name is independently dated and this work's title is not: the only public use of the title held here is an undated profile biography.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r34b2 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The work is Sun & Rain Works' conceptual designation connected to the consignment of lot 16 at Sotheby's Photographs, 2009; it is an assertion by the artist and is not recorded as a publicly witnessed performance.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r34b3 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object;
    rdfs:label "Artwork page";
    la:digitally_carried_by _:b0_r34b4.
_:b0_r34b4 <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/format> "text/html";
    a <http://www.ics.forth.gr/isl/CRMdig/D1_Digital_Object>;
    rdfs:label "Metaconceptual Art — WTF is Ansel Adams";
    la:access_point <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/artworks#work-wtf-is-ansel-adams>.
_:b0_r34b5 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "WTF is Ansel Adams";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r34b6 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "work:wtf-is-ansel-adams";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r34b7 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "A Metaconceptual Art work that Sun & Rain Works designates around its consignment of the Ansel Adams photograph 'San Francisco from Twin Peaks' (lot 16) to Sotheby's Photographs, 2009 (sale N08533, New York). Titled as a play on the auction and the canonical name of Ansel Adams, it treats the act of bringing the photograph to auction — its lot, its authority, its attribution — as the material of a conceptual work, alongside the born-digital work www.wtfisart.com. The work is a designation asserted by the artist and made explicit over time; it is not recorded as a publicly witnessed performance, and the Sotheby's catalogue lists the lot as an Ansel Adams photograph without naming Sun & Rain Works.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r34b8 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Documentation reported by Sun & Rain Works includes 2008 correspondence with Sotheby's Photographs, the public catalogue lot, photographs, and related records; these evidence the artist identity and the consignment. The fuller archive remains held by the studio pending redaction and rights review. This record asserts no historical-priority or precedence claim absent separately published evidence.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r34b9 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What the W stands for, in the artist's account. The studio states, in 2026, that the W of this title stands for 'Who', so that it reads 'Who the F is Ansel Adams', while the W of the companion work www.wtfisart.com stands for 'What'. No document from 2008 or 2009 spells out either expansion; this collection searched the correspondence of those years and the studio's working files and found none. The reading is the artist's and is recorded as that. What the choice does can be stated without adding to it: 'What' is the ordinary word of the idiom, so the companion work asks what art is, and 'Who' bends the idiom, so this work asks instead about a person - who the photographer is, and so what his name, stamp and signature do to a print whose identity rests on them. That gloss is this collection's and not the artist's.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r35b0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object;
    rdfs:label "Artwork page";
    la:digitally_carried_by _:b0_r35b1.
_:b0_r35b1 <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/format> "text/html";
    a <http://www.ics.forth.gr/isl/CRMdig/D1_Digital_Object>;
    rdfs:label "Metaconceptual Art — www.wtfisart.com Reconstruction";
    la:access_point <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/artworks/wtfisart-reconstruction>.
_:b0_r35b10 crm:P82a_begin_of_the_begin "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    crm:P82b_end_of_the_end "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    a crm:E52_Time-Span;
    rdfs:label "2026".
_:b0_r35b2 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "www.wtfisart.com — Reconstruction";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r35b3 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "work:wtfisart-reconstruction";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r35b4 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "A nine-page website built in 2026 that reconstructs www.wtfisart.com, the site Sun & Rain Works says it made in 2009, from the only two things that survive of it: the WTFIA folder of source documents assembled for it, and Growing Aloha, an unrelated website built by the same hands in the same months whose HTML, stylesheet and Dreamweaver template are intact. The content is WTFIA's; the form is Growing Aloha's. It is published as a work in its own right and is labelled as a reconstruction on every page.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r35b5 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What is transcribed, and what is inferred. Transcribed from the 2009 documents, unchanged: twenty-four dated press reports on the art market spanning 2000 to 2009, twenty-two of them carrying the source link the compiler recorded, with their headlines, bylines and excerpts; sixty-four Old Master dealers and galleries, each with the web address recorded for it in 2009; eighty-one modern and contemporary galleries grouped by state and country, for which the source document holds no web addresses; and a one-page note on the photographer Lee Balterman. Inferred, and belonging to the reconstruction rather than to 2009: the division into pages, the page titles, the order of the navigation column, the wording of the front page, the colour palette, and the existence of a page about the Ansel Adams consignment. Nothing in the surviving material records how the 2009 pages were arranged or what they said.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r35b6 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "How the form was derived. Growing Aloha, held on the same medium as the WTFIA folder and dated 2009-01-16 to 2009-01-20, is a Dreamweaver site built on the 'three column liquid, header and footer' starter layout: XHTML 1.0 Transitional, a .dwt template with named editable and repeating regions, the layout stylesheet inline in a style element wrapped in HTML comments, an Internet Explorer conditional comment supplying the hasLayout zoom fix, an external stylesheet of content-named classes, and a left column of block-display anchor links. Every one of those traits is reproduced here, and the reconstruction's stylesheet is modelled directly on Aloha.css, class for class. The reconstruction's own pages are valid XHTML 1.0 Transitional and carry Dreamweaver template-instance markers naming a template, Templates/WTFIA.dwt, which is published alongside them.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r35b7 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What was deliberately omitted. The 2009 sibling carried a Google Custom Search box and three AdSense units, and the surviving code contains the studio's own publisher and partner identifiers; the live third-party script and those identifiers are not reproduced, and the slots are marked where they sat. The art-historical plates and art-market press pictures in the WTFIA folder are third-party material held back pending rights review and are not shown. The 2009 photographs documenting the Ansel Adams print in the storage unit show an identifiable person and are withheld pending consent; the single photograph reproduced, the print's verso, is the studio's own and was already published. Growing Aloha's tiled background photograph belongs to an unrelated project and is not carried over. The press excerpts that are shown are short passages reproduced for commentary, each attributed to its author and publication and linked to its source.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r35b8 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Scope. This is a reconstruction and is evidence of nothing about the 2009 website beyond what the source documents already evidence. It is not a recovered copy: no HTML from www.wtfisart.com survives in the studio's archive or is known to exist elsewhere, and the WTFIA evidence record states that the folder holds what was made for the site rather than what the site published. It does not show that the site went live in 2009. The earliest Internet Archive capture of the domain, from February 2011, is a parked advertising page, which is evidence about February 2011 and not about 2009: the registrar's receipts show a one-year registration taken on 2009-03-16 and no renewal of it in the year that followed, so by February 2011 the domain had lapsed, and a parked page is what a registrar commonly serves at a lapsed address. The same receipts show the studio registering the name again on 2011-10-16, eight months after that capture, which bears on what became of the domain afterwards and not on what stood at it in 2009. What those receipts do establish is that the domain existed and was hosted from 2009-03-16; what stood at it in 2009, if anything, is evidenced by nothing. The studio's account that this site and the Sotheby's consignment were companion works is recorded as the artist's account: the dates are consistent with it, the latest material in the folder having been last saved a week before the sale, but no document in the folder mentions the consignment, and consistency is not intent. No precedence over any other artist, work, or event is claimed.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r35b9 crm:P14_carried_out_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    crm:P16_used_specific_object mca:evidence-wtfia-2009;
    crm:P4_has_time-span _:b0_r35b10;
    a crm:E65_Creation;
    rdfs:label "Creation of the www.wtfisart.com reconstruction".
_:b0_r36b0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object;
    rdfs:label "Artwork page";
    la:digitally_carried_by _:b0_r36b1.
_:b0_r36b1 <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/format> "text/html";
    a <http://www.ics.forth.gr/isl/CRMdig/D1_Digital_Object>;
    rdfs:label "Metaconceptual Art - www.wtfisart.com";
    la:access_point <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/artworks/wtfisart>.
_:b0_r36b10 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What the W stands for, in the artist's account. The studio states, in 2026, that the W of this work's name stands for 'What', so that it reads 'What the F is Art', while the W of the companion work WTF is Ansel Adams stands for 'Who'. No document from 2008 or 2009 spells out the expansion, and the reading is recorded as the artist's. It is consistent with the double reading recorded above: 'What' is the idiom's ordinary word, and the name is both the question what art is and the statement that it is that.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r36b11 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The work of the 2009 origin claim. This collection's origin claim for the 2009 engagement previously named a titled work, 'The Auction as Artwork', of which this website was modelled as an associated part. There was no such titled work, and the correction is set out in the record for the concept. The claim now names www.wtfisart.com, as the better dated of the studio's two works around the engagement: the registrar's receipt of 2009-03-16, billed to Sun and Rain Works, fixes the name fourteen days before the sale, on a document the studio did not write. WTF is Ansel Adams is recorded as the related work.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r36b12 crm:P14_carried_out_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    crm:P15_was_influenced_by mca:auction-as-artwork;
    crm:P4_has_time-span _:b0_r36b13;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by _:b0_r36b14;
    crm:P9i_forms_part_of mca:production-live-auction-2009;
    a crm:E65_Creation;
    rdfs:label "Creation of www.wtfisart.com".
_:b0_r36b13 crm:P82a_begin_of_the_begin "2009-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    crm:P82b_end_of_the_end "2009-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    a crm:E52_Time-Span;
    rdfs:label "2009".
_:b0_r36b14 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The website was built by Sun & Rain Works around 2009 in connection with its Sotheby's Photographs auction engagement; it is not recorded as having been authored live in the auction room.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r36b2 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "www.wtfisart.com";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r36b3 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "work:wtfisart";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r36b4 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "https://www.wtfisart.com/";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404626;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r36b5 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "A born-digital Metaconceptual Art work: a website built and owned by Sun & Rain Works around 2009, in connection with its Sotheby's Photographs auction engagement (sale N08533). Sun & Rain Works designates it as a work realising the concept of the auction as artwork.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r36b6 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "www.wtfisart.com is associated by Sun & Rain Works with the concept of the auction as artwork, in which Sun & Rain Works designates its 2009 Sotheby's Photographs auction engagement as art. The designation is an assertion by the artist.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r36b7 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Sun & Rain Works reports domain-registration and hosting receipts, site metadata, and related records documenting the website's existence and date. The fuller archive remains held by the studio pending redaction and rights review.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r36b8 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What the public record of the domain shows, checked 2026-09-01. The Verisign registry gives wtfisart.com a registration event of 2024-08-29 under the handle 2912143398_DOMAIN_COM-VRSN, with nameservers at a shared hosting provider. A renewal does not reset a creation date, so a registration event of that date on a freshly issued handle means the domain lapsed at some earlier point and was registered again as a new object. The consequence is that the original creation date is no longer in the public registry and cannot be recovered from it. The Internet Archive holds no capture of the domain before 2011-02-10 21:34:22 UTC, which is its earliest; that capture is a parked advertising page carrying no studio content, which is what a registrar commonly serves at an address whose registration has lapsed, and it is therefore evidence about February 2011 rather than about 2009. Commercial historical-WHOIS services were not consulted: the free interfaces to them are gated behind human-verification challenges. The date the studio first registered this domain is therefore not recoverable from the registry, but it is no longer undetermined: the registrar's own receipts, described in the hosting-receipts record, give a registration of WTFISART.COM on 2009-03-16 at 00:45:06 UTC and hosting bought for it the same night, with that hosting paid monthly to at least 2009-12-15. Those are a third party's business records rather than the studio's assertion. What stood at the address in 2009 remains unevidenced.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r36b9 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The name reads two ways. Written without spaces or punctuation, wtfisart is both a question, 'WTF is art?', and a statement, 'WTF is art'. The studio holds, in its account given in 2026, that the double reading was deliberate and that registering the name declared the work art. The registrar's receipt of 2009-03-16, billed to Sun and Rain Works, independently dates the name; the intent is the studio's statement, and the hosting-receipts record sets out the distinction.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r3b0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object;
    rdfs:label "Artwork page";
    la:digitally_carried_by _:b0_r3b1.
_:b0_r3b1 <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/format> "text/html";
    a <http://www.ics.forth.gr/isl/CRMdig/D1_Digital_Object>;
    rdfs:label "Metaconceptual Art — The auction as artwork (concept)";
    la:access_point <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/artworks#work-auction-as-artwork>.
_:b0_r3b2 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The auction as artwork (concept)";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r3b3 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "work:auction-as-artwork";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r3b4 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "A correction to what this record is. Until 2026-09-22 this collection recorded 'The Auction as Artwork' as a titled work, and modelled the two works around the 2009 engagement as its parts. The studio has stated that there was never a work of that title: the works are WTF is Ansel Adams and www.wtfisart.com, and the auction as artwork is the concept they share. The records agree with the studio. The phrase first appears in this collection on 2026-07-10, in the commit that first modelled the engagement, and no dated document uses it - not the 2008 and 2009 correspondence, not the postings of 2011 and 2012, not the studio's archived websites, and not the 2013 letter to a university museum, which calls the sale 'a performance act of Metaconceptual Art' and gives it no title at all. The title was therefore this collection's, introduced by its data model, and not the artist's. The record keeps its identifier, because that identifier has been published, cited and archived, and is retyped from a made object to a concept. Two consequences follow. The origin claim for 2009 now names www.wtfisart.com as its work, the better dated of the two, whose name the registrar dates to 2009-03-16, fourteen days before the sale; WTF is Ansel Adams is recorded as the related work. And nothing about what is evidenced changes: the concept remains the studio's assertion, and its earliest dated statement remains 2013.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r3b5 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The letter that gives the auction no title gives two other works one. The correction above rests in part on the 2013 letter to a university museum, which describes the sale as 'a performance act of Metaconceptual Art' and gives it no title. That is worth more than an absence, because the same letter titles other work in the same breath: 'Two popular examples of Metaconceptual Art, which are ongoing through time and space as well, are titled \"Art Market Reform\" , and \"Art Tax\"'. So the writer was naming works when he had names for them, in his own words, four years after the sale, and did not name this one. The argument for this record is therefore not only that no document uses the phrase 'The Auction as Artwork'; it is that the one document in which the studio lists its titled works, and mentions the auction, titles the works and not the auction.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r3b6 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "A concept of Sun & Rain Works, and not a work, formed in connection with the Sotheby's Photographs auction of 2009 (sale N08533, New York), at which Sun & Rain Works consigned an Ansel Adams photograph, 'San Francisco from Twin Peaks' (lot 16). Following the logic of the readymade, Sun & Rain Works frames its engagement with that public institutional event — its mechanism of value, authority, and sale — as the material of art. This is a designation asserted by the artist and made explicit over time; it is not a publicly witnessed live-creation event, and the Sotheby's catalogue does not name Sun & Rain Works. The concept is realised in two works, the website www.wtfisart.com and WTF is Ansel Adams; it is not itself a titled work, and the correction above sets out how this collection came to record it as one.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r3b7 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Documentation reported by Sun & Rain Works includes 2008 correspondence with Sotheby's Photographs, the public catalogue lot, photographs, receipts, and related records; these evidence the artist identity and the consignment. The fuller archive remains held by the studio pending redaction and rights review. This record makes no adjudicated historical-priority claim and asserts no comparison with, or precedence over, any later artist or event absent separately published evidence.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r4b0 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Metaconceptual Art — Collection";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r4b1 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "set:collection";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r4b2 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The museum is not a building; it is a set of relations. This Set gathers the works on view and the citable Movement Record as members so the collection can be read and queried as linked data. Membership is declared on each object via member_of.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r4b3 crm:P14_carried_out_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    crm:P4_has_time-span _:b0_r4b4;
    a crm:E65_Creation;
    rdfs:label "Assembly of the Metaconceptual Art collection".
_:b0_r4b4 crm:P82a_begin_of_the_begin "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    crm:P82b_end_of_the_end "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    a crm:E52_Time-Span;
    rdfs:label "2026".
_:b0_r5b0 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Metaconceptual Art";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r5b1 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "concept:metaconceptual-art";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r5b2 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Art that treats art, its institutions, markets, archives, and viewers as the material of the work. A self-defined movement concept created by Sun & Rain Works in 2007, extending conceptual art into the system that makes art legible. Its public Record argues credibility through genealogy, defined principles, works on view, machine-readable standards, external alignment, and provenance over time.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r5b3 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The 2007 creation date is asserted by Sun & Rain Works and is not directly evidenced. The earliest public use of the term located to date is a posting of 2011-11-28, which already treats it as a settled description, and the eight-part definition was posted publicly on 2012-11-22; both are described in the 2011-2012 posting evidence record and are readable on a third-party platform. A written declaration follows on 2013-09-12, delivered to a university art museum. The studio's 2008-2009 working archive contains no occurrence of the word, so the interval between the asserted origin and the first documented use remains open.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r5b4 crm:P14_carried_out_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    crm:P15_was_influenced_by aat:300047869, aat:300055863, aat:300264827, aat:300391330, aat:300419940, wd:Q18692990, wd:Q6041145;
    crm:P4_has_time-span _:b0_r5b5;
    a crm:E65_Creation;
    rdfs:label "Definition of Metaconceptual Art as a movement concept".
_:b0_r5b5 crm:P82a_begin_of_the_begin "2007-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    crm:P82b_end_of_the_end "2007-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    a crm:E52_Time-Span;
    rdfs:label "2007".
_:b0_r6b0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object;
    rdfs:label "Artwork page";
    la:digitally_carried_by _:b0_r6b1.
_:b0_r6b1 <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/format> "text/html";
    a <http://www.ics.forth.gr/isl/CRMdig/D1_Digital_Object>;
    rdfs:label "Metaconceptual Art — Artworks";
    la:access_point <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/artworks#work-construction-museum>.
_:b0_r6b2 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Construction of the Museum as Concept";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r6b3 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "work:construction-museum";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r6b4 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "An image study in which the museum appears inside the artwork as something still being built. Institutional authority is shown as architecture, ornament, labor, scaffolding, and frame.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r6b5 crm:P14_carried_out_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    crm:P4_has_time-span _:b0_r6b6;
    a crm:E65_Creation;
    rdfs:label "Creation of Construction of the Museum as Concept".
_:b0_r6b6 crm:P82a_begin_of_the_begin "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    crm:P82b_end_of_the_end "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    a crm:E52_Time-Span;
    rdfs:label "2026".
_:b0_r7b0 crm:P2_has_type <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/>;
    crm:P75i_is_possessed_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    a crm:E30_Right;
    rdfs:label "CC BY 4.0".
_:b0_r7b1 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object;
    rdfs:label "Artwork page";
    la:digitally_carried_by _:b0_r7b2.
_:b0_r7b2 <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/format> "text/html";
    a <http://www.ics.forth.gr/isl/CRMdig/D1_Digital_Object>;
    rdfs:label "Metaconceptual Art — Artworks";
    la:access_point <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/artworks#work-eight-sentences>.
_:b0_r7b3 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Eight Sentences On Metaconceptual Art";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r7b4 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "work:eight-sentences";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r7b5 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "A canonical eight-sentence proposition set framing Metaconceptual Art as a field of form, institution, market, history, medium, access, and life. Written after Sol LeWitt's Sentences on Conceptual Art.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r7b6 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The sequence has a documented public form predating this site by fourteen years. It was posted in full, as 'The 8 Defining characteristics of Metaconceptual Art', on 2012-11-22, and the text published here agrees with that posting word for word apart from a comma, a space, and the spelling of 'Transcendance', since normalised. The posting is described in the 2011-2012 posting evidence record. The stated debt to Sol LeWitt does not appear in the 2012 text and is a later framing.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r7b7 crm:P14_carried_out_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    crm:P15_was_influenced_by ulan:500115429;
    crm:P4_has_time-span _:b0_r7b8;
    a crm:E65_Creation;
    rdfs:label "Writing of the Eight Sentences".
_:b0_r7b8 crm:P82a_begin_of_the_begin "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    crm:P82b_end_of_the_end "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    a crm:E52_Time-Span;
    rdfs:label "2026".
_:b0_r8b0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object;
    rdfs:label "Artwork page";
    la:digitally_carried_by _:b0_r8b1.
_:b0_r8b1 <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/format> "text/html";
    a <http://www.ics.forth.gr/isl/CRMdig/D1_Digital_Object>;
    rdfs:label "Metaconceptual Art — Eightfold Sprocket";
    la:access_point <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/artworks/eightfold-sprocket>.
_:b0_r8b2 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Eightfold Sprocket";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r8b3 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "work:eightfold-sprocket";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r8b4 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "An emblem for the eight-sentence proposition set: a small mechanical sign for repetition, rotation, and system.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r8b5 crm:P14_carried_out_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    crm:P4_has_time-span _:b0_r8b6;
    a crm:E65_Creation;
    rdfs:label "Creation of Eightfold Sprocket".
_:b0_r8b6 crm:P82a_begin_of_the_begin "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    crm:P82b_end_of_the_end "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    a crm:E52_Time-Span;
    rdfs:label "2026".
_:b0_r9b0 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Evidence archive: the approach to the Ansel Adams organisation (8-9 August 2008)";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300404670;
    crm:P72_has_language aat:300388277;
    a crm:E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation.
_:b0_r9b1 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "record:evidence-adams-gallery-2008";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300312355;
    a crm:E42_Identifier.
_:b0_r9b10 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The sign-off, moved back. The valediction for the love of Art closes both messages of 2008-08-08, and so predates by two days the earliest previously documented use of it. The auction-house record treats the phrase at length, as the studio's own adaptation of the title of Damien Hirst's For the Love of God, and observes that it closed both approaches of August 2008 and was therefore a settled signature rather than a one-off. That observation is strengthened: the phrase now closes four messages, to three organisations, across six days, and is present in the earliest documents the collection holds. What it is not, here as there, is a reference anyone was invited to see. The studio's account of the Hirst adaptation is its own, given in 2026, and is reported as that account rather than as a fact these messages establish.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r9b11 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "What these documents establish, and what they do not. They establish that by the evening of 2008-08-08 the studio principal held the Ansel Adams print, had photographed it earlier that day, and was sending those photographs to the organisation bearing the photographer's name; that the archive later sent to the auction house existed in its final form by that evening; that the contact followed an earlier approach made by telephone; and that the sign-off used throughout the August 2008 outreach was in use from the earliest documented moment. They do not touch Metaconceptual Art, which is named in neither. They do not describe the print, the photographs or the approach as an artwork, a performance or a project of any kind: one message offers assistance, the other asks after the recipient and sends files. And they carry no designation of the auction, which had been proposed to nobody when they were written.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r9b12 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Redaction and scope. Following this collection's convention, the representative at the Adams organisation, who was addressed in an official capacity, is identified by role only; their given name and mailbox are withheld pending consent and retained in the studio's private copy. Both correspondents' mailbox addresses are replaced, and the studio principal's telephone numbers are withheld as personal data, though the fact that two numbers are given, that they differ in one digit, and that one is given 'again', is preserved because it bears on the order of the messages. The final octet of the sending provider's X-Originating-IP stamp is withheld; the header itself and the rest of its value are retained, because the presence of that stamp is what distinguishes the two files' provenance and is the point of the record. The domain anseladams.com is deliberately retained: the identity of the organisation approached is what gives the record its weight. No endorsement by that organisation of this project, of Metaconceptual Art, or of any claim in this collection is implied or should be inferred. It has not been approached about either message and has said nothing about them. The unredacted originals are not published; their SHA-256 values are, so the held files can later be shown to be unaltered without their contents being disclosed.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r9b13 crm:P14_carried_out_by mca:sun-and-rain-works;
    crm:P4_has_time-span _:b0_r9b14;
    a crm:E65_Creation;
    rdfs:label "Compilation of the Adams organisation approach record".
_:b0_r9b14 crm:P82a_begin_of_the_begin "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    crm:P82b_end_of_the_end "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime>;
    a crm:E52_Time-Span;
    rdfs:label "2026".
_:b0_r9b2 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Two outbound messages to the same named representative at anseladams.com, sent within about twenty minutes of each other on the evening of 2008-08-08 Pacific time, from the studio principal's then-current hotmail.com mailbox. The first as the headers order them, under the subject 'Ansel Adams San Francisco', carries eight loose JPEG photographs of the Ansel Adams print. The second, under the subject 'Ansel Adams Visits SanFrancisco', carries a single attachment: the archive San Francisco.zip, byte-identical to the copy the studio holds and to the one sent to the auction house six days later. Both describe the camera as a 1.3 megapixel FujiFilm, both invite a telephone call in preference to email, both give a telephone number, and both close for the love of Art. Together they are the earliest documents held in this collection, preceding the approach to the museum photography department by two days and the approach to the auction house by five.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r9b3 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The two messages are not equally well evidenced, and the difference is the most important thing in this record. The message carrying the archive retains its original Hotmail headers: an X-Originating-IP stamped by the sending provider, a Date bearing a real local offset of minus seven hours, a GUID-style MIME boundary of the kind Windows Live Hotmail generated, and no Message-ID, Thread-Index, Content-Language or Exchange organisation headers at all. The message carrying the loose photographs has the opposite profile: its Date is normalised to UTC, and it carries a Message-ID whose host is Exchange Online, together with Thread-Index and X-MS-Exchange-Organization headers. Exchange Online did not exist in 2008. So one of these two files has passed through a later platform that rewrote its headers, and the other has not. The archive message is, on its headers, a 2008 artefact. The photographs message is a 2008 message as preserved by a present-day mailbox.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r9b4 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "A period marker in the archive message, stated for what it is worth. Its footer advertises the Windows Live Hotmail Mobile Summer Games Trivia Contest, linking to gowindowslive.com. The Beijing Olympic Games opened on 2008-08-08, the date the message bears, and ran to 2008-08-24. A promotional footer tied to that event is consistent with the stated date and would be an odd thing to reconstruct afterwards. It is corroboration of period rather than of the particular day, and it is not independent of the sender, since the footer travelled with the message he sent.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r9b5 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Their order is not settled, and this record does not settle it. The headers place the photographs message about twenty minutes before the archive message. The texts point the other way. The archive message reads as a first delivery: it opens with a greeting and an enquiry after the recipient, describes the photographs as the best the writer could manage, and states a telephone number plainly. The photographs message reads as a second attempt: it says the number is being given 'again', and sends eight of the eleven frames loose rather than archived, which is what a sender does when an archive has not worked. Against that, the two messages give telephone numbers differing in a single digit, so one of them contains a typing error and neither can be used to order them. The honest position is that two messages were sent to the same person within twenty-odd minutes on one evening, that the timestamp of one of them has been rewritten by a later system, and that which came first is not established. Nothing in this collection depends on the answer.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r9b6 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The archive ties this approach to the auction-house record, and does so by content rather than by assertion. The attachment to the 2008-08-08 message is byte-identical to San Francisco.zip, whose SHA-256 the auction-house record already published as the 3 MB attachment sent to the Photographs department on 2008-08-14. The same archive therefore went to the Ansel Adams organisation six days before it went to the trade. Its eleven frames carry EXIF DateTimeOriginal values running from 2008-08-08 00:05:33 to 00:10:22, a single session of about five minutes; the EXIF camera model is FUJIFILM FinePix1300, which agrees with both messages' description of a 1.3 megapixel camera. The eight loose photographs in the other message are byte-identical to eight members of that same archive. Three frames, DSCF0014, DSCF0015 and DSCF0023, were not sent loose. DSCF0020, the frame composing the print with Warhol's philosophy, was sent both ways.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r9b7 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "It answers an open question in the museum record, and the two records corroborate each other. The museum letter of 2008-08-11 reports that its writer is in contact with a named person at what he calls 'the Ansel Adams Musem in SanFrancisco'. The museum record notes that no institution of that name was operating in San Francisco in 2008, the Ansel Adams Center for Photography having closed in 2001, and left open which body was meant. These messages identify it, and the museum letter, now held as a file, names the person in full, so the identification no longer rests on inference at all. They are addressed to a named representative at anseladams.com and they predate the museum letter, which is the order that letter's own past tense requires. The misnaming is resolved as a misnaming: the organisation approached bore the photographer's name and was not a San Francisco museum. The weight lies in the independence of the artefacts. The museum letter is held as a screenshot of a mail client; these are held as mailbox exports. Neither was produced from the other, neither needed to mention the other, and they agree on a checkable fact.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r9b8 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Contact began before either message. The photographs message gives a telephone number 'again', and the archive message opens 'How are you doing', asks nothing and explains nothing about who is writing or why, and offers the photographs as though they had been asked for. Both presume a relationship already established. So the engagement with the Adams organisation began before 2008-08-08, and on the evidence of both messages it began by telephone. Nothing of that earlier contact is held: no note, no call record, no reply from the organisation to anything. The earliest documents in this collection are therefore not the beginning of the engagement they document, and no claim about a first approach should rest on them.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
_:b0_r9b9 crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "The subject lines, and what is not yet in them. These two messages are headed 'Ansel Adams San Francisco' and 'Ansel Adams Visits SanFrancisco'. The messages of 2008-08-11 and 2008-08-14, to the museum and to the auction house, are headed 'Ansel Adams Finds Art in SanFrancisco', and the auction house's replies carry that subject forward. So across six days the heading moves from a plain statement of the subject, through a personification of the photographer as a visitor, to a claim that he finds Art there. The progression is recorded and not interpreted. It is consistent with a framing that developed over that week, and equally consistent with a writer trying different headings on different correspondents; nothing held distinguishes the two. The art-inflected heading is not present at the earliest date.";
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300435416;
    a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object.
